
export const sciencePrompts = [

  // --- 1. RESEARCH DESIGN & METHODOLOGY (sci-1 to sci-10) ---
  {"id":"sci-1","title":"Power Calculation for a Two-Arm RCT","prompt":"You are a senior biostatistician at a clinical trials unit designing a two-arm parallel-group randomised controlled trial testing whether a sodium-glucose cotransporter-2 (SGLT2) inhibitor reduces 24-hour urinary albumin-to-creatinine ratio (UACR) in adults with early diabetic nephropathy compared to placebo. Using G*Power 3.1, walk through a complete a priori sample size calculation: state the primary outcome (UACR log-transformed, continuous), specify the minimally clinically important difference (40% reduction in geometric mean UACR based on the CREDENCE trial data), the anticipated pooled standard deviation (0.55 log units from pilot data), alpha = 0.025 one-tailed (pre-specified superiority test), and power = 90%. Adjust for a 20% dropout rate and 5% non-compliance rate. Produce a formatted paragraph suitable for inclusion in a trial protocol and a CONSORT-compatible flow diagram described in text showing allocation, follow-up, and analysis populations. Explain how a sensitivity analysis varying the assumed SD from 0.45 to 0.65 would affect the required N.","tags":["research-design","statistics","clinical-trials","RCT","power-calculation"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":true},

  {"id":"sci-2","title":"Factorial Design for Behavioural Neuroscience","prompt":"You are a research methods consultant advising a PhD student in behavioural neuroscience who wants to test whether housing enrichment and voluntary exercise independently and synergistically improve spatial memory in 12-month-old male C57BL/6J mice. Design a fully factorial 2×2 between-subjects experiment (standard cage vs enriched cage × sedentary vs running wheel access) with n=12 per cell. Specify: the primary outcome measure (Barnes maze escape latency, days 1-5 acquisition plus probe trial), secondary measures (open field locomotion as covariate, hippocampal BDNF protein by ELISA), randomisation procedure (block randomisation by litter), blinding strategy for outcome assessment, statistical analysis plan (two-way ANOVA with interaction term, effect size as partial η², post-hoc Tukey HSD with Bonferroni correction for secondary outcomes), and the key assumption of homogeneity of variance to be tested with Levene's test before proceeding. Explain what a significant interaction term would mean biologically and how you would visualise it.","tags":["research-design","factorial-design","neuroscience","ANOVA","animal-models"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:05:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-3","title":"Minimising Selection Bias in Cohort Studies","prompt":"You are an epidemiologist at a school of public health designing a prospective cohort study to examine whether long-term particulate matter (PM2.5) exposure above WHO guidelines (15 µg/m³ annual mean) increases the incidence of type 2 diabetes over a 10-year follow-up in adults aged 40-65 residing in urban areas. Identify and operationalise strategies to minimise four distinct forms of selection bias: healthy worker effect, loss to follow-up bias, volunteer bias, and prevalent-case bias. For each bias type, explain the mechanism by which it would distort the exposure-outcome association, quantify the direction of the likely bias (towards or away from the null), and describe the specific design or analytical strategy used to address it. Then write a 200-word Strengths and Limitations paragraph for the Discussion section of the protocol paper that addresses all four biases honestly and in the style of an environmental health journal such as Environmental Health Perspectives.","tags":["research-design","epidemiology","selection-bias","cohort-study","environmental-health"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:10:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-4","title":"Crossover Trial Design with Washout Periods","prompt":"You are a clinical pharmacologist designing a four-period crossover pharmacokinetic study to compare the bioavailability of a new oral formulation of ibuprofen (200 mg soft-gel capsule) versus the reference standard tablet under fasted and fed conditions in 24 healthy adult volunteers. Specify the Williams square Latin square sequence to balance treatment order, justify the 7-day washout period based on ibuprofen's half-life of approximately 2 hours and the 5-half-life rule, describe the PK sampling schedule (0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1, 1.5, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 12 hours post-dose), define the primary PK endpoints (AUC0-∞ and Cmax) and the bioequivalence acceptance criteria (90% CI of the geometric mean ratio within 80-125%), explain how you would test for and handle a significant carryover effect using a mixed-effects model for repeated measures (MMRM) in SAS PROC MIXED, and outline the EMA bioequivalence guideline requirements this design must satisfy.","tags":["research-design","crossover-trial","pharmacokinetics","bioequivalence","clinical-pharmacology"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:15:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-5","title":"Controls Strategy for a CRISPR Screen","prompt":"You are a functional genomics researcher at a cancer centre planning a genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 dropout screen in K562 chronic myeloid leukaemia cells using the Brunello library (77,441 sgRNAs targeting 19,114 genes, 4 guides/gene). Design the complete controls strategy: specify non-targeting control guides (n=1000), positive control essential genes (RPS12, RPL13A, PCNA — explain why these are used), negative control non-essential genes (HPRT1 in female cells as a pseudo-essential), and cell-lethal positive controls (CDK1). Describe how to calculate a library coverage of at least 500× per sgRNA and justify the cell number at each passage. Explain how MAGeCK-MLE will be used to calculate log fold-change scores and beta scores, and how the non-targeting controls define the null distribution for FDR calculation. Discuss how to detect guide depletion versus enrichment screens and which Cas9 expression validation step (T7E1 or Sanger sequencing) must precede the screen.","tags":["research-design","CRISPR","functional-genomics","screen-design","controls"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:20:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-6","title":"Adaptive Trial Design Framework","prompt":"You are a clinical trial statistician advising an oncology team that wants to design a Phase II adaptive seamless dose-finding and efficacy trial for a novel PD-L1 inhibitor in treatment-refractory microsatellite-stable colorectal cancer. Explain the key features of a Bayesian adaptive design using a Continual Reassessment Method (CRM) for dose escalation in Stage 1 (three dose levels: 3, 10, 30 mg/kg IV q3w, n=18) that seamlessly transitions to a randomised Stage 2 comparing the selected dose versus standard-of-care FOLFIRI. Specify: the prior distribution on the dose-toxicity curve, the target DLT rate of 25%, the stopping rules for futility (predictive probability of success below 5%) and safety (two DLTs at same dose level), the type I error control using alpha spending with an O'Brien-Fleming boundary across two interim analyses, and the pre-specified decision criteria for dose selection. Explain why a traditional 3+3 design would be inferior for this indication and what FDA guidance documents (e.g., FDA Adaptive Designs guidance 2019) apply.","tags":["research-design","adaptive-trial","Bayesian","oncology","dose-finding"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:25:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-7","title":"Measurement Invariance Testing in Psychology Research","prompt":"You are a psychometrician reviewing a cross-cultural study that compares depression symptom severity measured by the PHQ-9 across three cultural groups (UK-born White British, UK-born British South Asian, and first-generation South Asian immigrants, n=250 per group). The researchers want to use a one-way ANCOVA to compare mean PHQ-9 scores and claim that the South Asian immigrant group scores significantly higher. Before accepting the comparison, walk through the full measurement invariance testing sequence using confirmatory factor analysis (CFA) in R with the lavaan package: configural model, metric model (equal factor loadings), scalar model (equal intercepts), and strict model (equal residuals). Explain what each step tests, how to evaluate fit differences using CFI change (ΔCFI > 0.01) and RMSEA, and what partial scalar invariance means for the substantive interpretation of the group comparison. Recommend whether the ANCOVA is justified and, if not, what alternative analysis approach preserves validity.","tags":["research-design","psychometrics","measurement-invariance","CFA","cross-cultural"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:30:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-8","title":"Dose-Response Curve Fitting and EC50 Estimation","prompt":"You are a pharmacologist helping a research team fit dose-response data from a cell viability assay in which MCF-7 cells were treated with a novel CDK4/6 inhibitor across 10 concentrations (0.001 to 100 µM, half-log spacing, n=6 replicate wells per concentration, three independent experiments). Walk through the complete analytical workflow: confirm the data follow a sigmoidal dose-response relationship by plotting raw viability (%) against log10[concentration] in GraphPad Prism 10, fit a four-parameter logistic (4PL) model specifying Top, Bottom, HillSlope, and LogEC50 as free parameters, explain how to constrain Bottom to ≥0 and Top to ≤100 for a normalised assay, describe how to calculate EC50, EC90, and the selectivity index relative to non-cancerous MCF-10A cells, report goodness-of-fit using R² and sum of squares, and explain when a three-parameter model is more appropriate. Address how to handle a Hill slope significantly different from 1.0 and what it implies about binding cooperativity or assay artefacts.","tags":["research-design","pharmacology","dose-response","EC50","GraphPad-Prism"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:35:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-9","title":"IRB Protocol: Sensitive Research with Vulnerable Populations","prompt":"You are a research ethics consultant helping a team design an IRB/ethics committee application for a study examining trauma histories and PTSD symptom severity in unaccompanied asylum-seeking adolescents aged 14-17 currently in temporary government housing in the UK. Write the Risk-Benefit Assessment and Safeguarding sections of the ethics application. For the risk assessment, enumerate five specific risks (re-traumatisation, confidentiality breach, interpreter bias, power imbalance with researcher, risk of identifying information in qualitative data) and for each risk specify the mitigation strategy with reference to British Psychological Society Ethics Guidelines 2021. For the safeguarding section, describe the mandatory reporting threshold for disclosures of ongoing harm, the referral pathway, how this will be explained in the assent/consent process, and how to balance participant confidentiality with duty of care. Include a sentence on capacity assessment and Gillick competence for under-16 participants. Write at the level expected by a university research ethics committee.","tags":["research-design","ethics","IRB","vulnerable-populations","safeguarding"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:40:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-10","title":"Replication Study Design and Pre-Registration","prompt":"You are a research methodologist helping a team design a direct replication of a high-profile 2021 Nature Human Behaviour study that reported a single 20-minute nature walk reduced resting-state amygdala reactivity to negative images (measured by fMRI, n=38, d=0.61). Draft a complete pre-registration document for submission to the OSF (Open Science Framework) that includes: hypotheses stated in directional form, exact procedural details to be replicated (same stimuli set, same MRI acquisition parameters at 3T Siemens Prisma, same ROI analysis approach), a priori power calculation for 95% power to detect d=0.61 with a two-tailed t-test (yielding n=53 per group), the pre-specified primary analysis and decision criterion for replication success (effect size within original 95% CI AND p < 0.05), two planned equivalence tests using TOST if the effect is non-significant, and a sensitivity analysis plan for site-specific scanner differences. Explain the distinction between direct and conceptual replication and why this design qualifies as the former.","tags":["research-design","replication","pre-registration","OSF","fMRI"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:45:00Z","featured":false},

  // --- 2. SCIENTIFIC WRITING & PUBLICATION (sci-11 to sci-20) ---
  {"id":"sci-11","title":"Nature Methods Paper: Results Section Architecture","prompt":"You are a scientific writing coach working with a team preparing their first manuscript for Nature Methods describing a novel microfluidic device for single-cell western blotting that achieves 10-fold higher throughput than existing commercial platforms (Milo, ProteinSimple). Write the Results section architecture — the narrative spine — as a detailed outline with annotated paragraph-level summaries. Each result block should: open with a one-sentence claim (the finding), follow with a description of the experiment that tested it, state the quantitative result with statistical support, and close with a linking sentence to the next finding. The Results should progress from device fabrication and characterisation (feature resolution by SEM, 450 nm), to technical validation (comparison of protein quantification linearity against standard Western blot, R²=0.993), to biological application (profiling p53, phospho-ERK, and GAPDH in single MCF-7 cells after doxorubicin treatment, n=1,847 cells). Do not write placeholder text — write the actual claims and linking logic. Approximately 600 words.","tags":["scientific-writing","Nature-Methods","results-section","manuscript","microfluidics"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:50:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-12","title":"Abstract Revision: From Weak to Publication-Ready","prompt":"You are a scientific writing editor at a high-impact journal. [Paste your manuscript draft or abstract]\n\nRevise this abstract to meet the following standards enforced at journals such as PNAS, eLife, and Cell Reports: (1) the opening sentence must state the unresolved scientific problem, not broad background — delete any sentence beginning with 'X is important because'; (2) the Methods summary must specify the model system, key technique, and sample size in one sentence; (3) the primary quantitative result must appear with exact values, confidence intervals, and the direction of the effect; (4) the Conclusions sentence must be falsifiable and must not introduce any new concept not supported by the stated results; (5) hedged language ('we suggest', 'may', 'could potentially') must be reserved for genuinely uncertain mechanistic claims, not for clearly supported empirical findings; (6) word count must not exceed 250 words. Return the revised abstract with tracked changes described as a bullet list of specific edits and the editorial rationale for each.","tags":["scientific-writing","abstract","editing","journal-submission","peer-review"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:55:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-13","title":"Cover Letter to Cell for a High-Stakes Submission","prompt":"You are a corresponding author submitting a manuscript to Cell titled 'Somatic LINE-1 retrotransposition in human neurons drives transcriptional heterogeneity and accelerates Alzheimer's disease pathology'. Write a 380-word cover letter that meets Cell's submission requirements. The letter must: open with a single sentence establishing the fundamental biological significance of somatic retrotransposition in post-mitotic neurons (not a generic disease burden statistic), state the three principal findings (LINE-1 insertions accumulate 2.7-fold in AD neurons versus age-matched controls by single-cell whole-genome sequencing; insertion hotspots are enriched in synaptic gene loci; experimental LINE-1 activation in iPSC-derived neurons recapitulates tau hyperphosphorylation), explain why the mechanistic link between retrotransposition and tau pathology is a conceptual advance beyond existing neuroinflammation models, note the use of three independent cohorts (n=142 post-mortem brains total) and two orthogonal sequencing platforms, confirm no parallel submission, and close with a sentence about the broad readership interest for Cell. Precise, authoritative, not sycophantic.","tags":["scientific-writing","cover-letter","Cell","Alzheimer","journal-submission"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-14","title":"Discussion Section: Integrating Unexpected Findings","prompt":"You are an experienced scientific editor. Write a Discussion section (550-650 words) for a proteomics study that set out to identify biomarkers of early pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC) in plasma but produced an unexpected primary finding: the most significantly upregulated protein in PDAC plasma compared to healthy controls was not a known cancer antigen but ITIH4 (inter-alpha-trypsin inhibitor heavy chain 4), a liver-secreted acute-phase protein. Structure the Discussion as follows: paragraph 1 restates the key finding and acknowledges it diverged from the hypothesis; paragraph 2 situates ITIH4 in the existing acute-phase response literature and proposes two mechanistic explanations (liver reprogramming by tumour-secreted IL-6, complement pathway dysregulation); paragraph 3 compares performance of ITIH4 (AUC 0.84, 95% CI 0.77-0.91) against CA19-9 (AUC 0.71) for early-stage PDAC; paragraph 4 addresses three specific limitations (plasma proteome depletion artefacts, matched-pair design limitations, lack of longitudinal validation); paragraph 5 proposes next steps. Use appropriately hedged language and avoid the words 'novel', 'unique', and 'importantly'.","tags":["scientific-writing","discussion","proteomics","PDAC","biomarkers"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:05:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-15","title":"Structured CONSORT Abstract for a Surgical RCT","prompt":"You are a medical writer with expertise in CONSORT 2010 reporting. Write a structured 280-word abstract for a randomised controlled trial comparing laparoscopic sleeve gastrectomy (LSG) versus laparoscopic Roux-en-Y gastric bypass (LRYGB) for type 2 diabetes remission at 5 years in adults with BMI 30-35 kg/m² and poorly controlled T2DM (HbA1c ≥ 58 mmol/mol on ≥2 medications). Use the CONSORT extension for abstracts with bolded section headers: Background, Objectives, Trial Design, Participants, Interventions, Outcomes, Randomisation, Blinding, Numbers Analysed, Outcomes Results (5-year T2DM complete remission rate: LSG 47.3% vs LRYGB 62.1%, absolute risk difference 14.8%, 95% CI 6.2-23.4%, p=0.001; number needed to treat = 7), Harms (serious adverse events: LSG 8.4% vs LRYGB 12.7%), Conclusions, Trial Registration. Each section must contain only factual, quantified information — no padding sentences. The abstract must be reproducible: a reader should be able to understand the study design completely from the abstract alone.","tags":["scientific-writing","CONSORT","RCT","bariatric-surgery","clinical-trial"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:10:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-16","title":"Writing the Introduction: Funnel Logic and Citation Strategy","prompt":"You are a scientific writing coach. Write a complete Introduction section (500 words) for a paper on the use of extracellular vesicle (EV)-delivered antisense oligonucleotides (ASOs) to silence mutant HTT mRNA in Huntington's disease patient-derived striatal neurons. Follow the inverted-funnel structure: paragraph 1 (broad context: polyglutamine expansion disorders and the unmet therapeutic need in HD, citing landmark natural history data), paragraph 2 (existing ASO therapeutic approaches and their limitation of poor CNS penetration and immunogenicity when delivered systemically — cite ISIS-443139 Phase I/II trial data), paragraph 3 (EVs as endogenous delivery vehicles: tropism for the CNS, evasion of immune clearance, loading strategies), paragraph 4 (the specific unaddressed gap: no study has combined striatal-targeting EVs with HTT ASOs in patient-derived neurons with confirmed CAG expansion length), paragraph 5 (one-paragraph statement of the study objectives and experimental approach). Use numbered reference placeholders [1]-[14]. End with a sentence that makes the reader want to see the Results.","tags":["scientific-writing","introduction","Huntington","antisense-oligonucleotides","EV-delivery"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:15:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-17","title":"Authorship Dispute: Drafting a Professional Response","prompt":"You are a senior scientist and department head. A postdoctoral researcher has written to you claiming that the corresponding author of a recently submitted PNAS manuscript excluded her from the author list despite her performing 60% of the experiments described in Figures 2, 4, and 5, generating all the raw imaging data, and contributing to the Methods section draft. She has documentation (lab notebook entries, email threads, Benchling records). Draft a formal response email to both the postdoc and the corresponding author that: acknowledges the seriousness of the allegation without prejudging, references ICMJE authorship criteria (substantial contribution to conception/design/acquisition OR analysis; drafting or critically revising; final approval; accountability), requests a written response from the corresponding author within 10 working days with evidence, outlines the institutional investigation pathway under the UK Research Integrity Office (UKRIO) Code of Practice 2023, and clearly states that the journal editor will be notified if the authorship list cannot be resolved internally. Maintain a professional, neutral tone appropriate for a research integrity matter.","tags":["scientific-writing","authorship","research-integrity","ICMJE","publication-ethics"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:20:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-18","title":"Graphical Abstract Design Brief","prompt":"You are a scientific illustrator and journal editor. Write a detailed design brief for a graphical abstract to accompany a paper in Cell Chemical Biology describing a new class of bifunctional small molecules (PROTACs) that simultaneously degrade BRD4 and recruit STING to restore innate immune sensing in KRAS-mutant non-small cell lung cancer cells. The graphical abstract must convey the mechanism in three visual panels reading left to right: panel 1 (the problem — KRAS-mutant NSCLC cells with suppressed STING pathway and BRD4-driven MYC transcription, illustrated by a cancer cell with a crossed-out STING protein and an active transcription arrow), panel 2 (the PROTAC mechanism — bifunctional molecule binding BRD4 with JQ1 warhead and E3 ligase CRBN with thalidomide linker, forming a ternary complex leading to ubiquitin-proteasome degradation, plus simultaneous STING activation shown by cGAMP binding), panel 3 (the outcome — restored type I interferon secretion and T cell recruitment). Specify colour scheme (Cell standard palette), font sizes, which labels are mandatory, and the 350-word limit for accompanying legend text. Note which panel is most important for the 'one-glance understanding' criterion used by Cell editors.","tags":["scientific-writing","graphical-abstract","Cell","PROTAC","science-communication"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:25:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-19","title":"Preprint to Journal Submission: Upgrading a bioRxiv Draft","prompt":"You are a scientific editor preparing a bioRxiv preprint for submission to Nature Communications. The preprint on murine gut microbiome responses to a high-fibre diet received 12 comments on bioRxiv, including: (1) the 16S rRNA V4 region amplicon sequencing data are undeposited; (2) the Figure 3 PERMANOVA analysis lacks reporting of the R² statistic; (3) the Discussion overclaims causality from a correlational design. Write a structured revision checklist with three sections. Section A (Data and Reproducibility): list five specific actions required before submission including NCBI SRA deposition with accession number, GitHub repository for the QIIME2 analysis pipeline with version pinning, and a data availability statement template. Section B (Statistical Reporting): identify four specific figures where statistical reporting is incomplete and specify exactly what must be added (effect sizes, post-hoc comparisons, sample sizes in figure legends). Section C (Writing Quality): identify three specific phrases from the Discussion that overstate causality and provide a revised version of each phrase with appropriate hedging for a cross-sectional microbiome study. Format as a practical checklist for the author.","tags":["scientific-writing","preprint","bioRxiv","Nature-Communications","revision"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:30:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-20","title":"Methods Section: Rigorous Reporting for Single-Cell RNA-Seq","prompt":"You are a methods editor at Nature Genetics. Write a complete Methods subsection (600 words) for a single-cell RNA sequencing study profiling 47,000 cells from fresh human pancreatic islet tissue from 12 donors (6 type 2 diabetic, 6 normoglycaemic controls) processed on a 10x Genomics Chromium Next GEM Single Cell 3' kit v3.1. Include the following with enough precision for full reproducibility: tissue dissociation protocol (collagenase P 0.5 mg/mL digestion, 37°C, 15 min with trituration, viability threshold >85% by trypan blue), cell loading and capture (target 10,000 cells per lane, 8 donors multiplexed with TotalSeq-C hashtag antibodies), sequencing parameters (NovaSeq 6000, paired-end 28×98 cycles, minimum 25,000 reads per cell), quality control thresholds in Seurat v5.0 (minimum 500 genes per cell, maximum 20% mitochondrial reads, doublet removal with DoubletFinder), normalisation (SCTransform v2 with regression of mitochondrial percentage and cell cycle score), clustering (Leiden algorithm, resolution 0.5, 40 PCs), and cell type annotation strategy (marker genes and SingleR with HumanPrimaryCellAtlasData reference). State the genome reference (GRCh38 GENCODE v43).","tags":["scientific-writing","methods","scRNA-seq","single-cell","genomics"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:35:00Z","featured":false},

  // --- 3. LITERATURE REVIEW & SYNTHESIS (sci-21 to sci-30) ---
  {"id":"sci-21","title":"PRISMA-Compliant Systematic Review Protocol","prompt":"You are a systematic review methodologist at a Cochrane Collaboration–affiliated centre. Draft a complete protocol for a systematic review and meta-analysis examining whether mindfulness-based cognitive therapy (MBCT) reduces relapse rates in adults with recurrent major depressive disorder compared to treatment-as-usual (TAU) or antidepressant maintenance therapy at 12-month follow-up. Follow PRISMA-P 2015 reporting guidelines and include: background rationale citing the Kuyken et al. 2015 Lancet meta-analysis and its limitations, the PICO question in structured form, pre-specified eligibility criteria (RCTs only, DSM-5/ICD-10 diagnosis, minimum 8-week MBCT programme), search strategy with a worked PubMed Boolean string (MeSH terms and free text), databases (PubMed, PsycINFO, CENTRAL, EMBASE, CINAHL, grey literature via ProQuest Dissertations), data extraction form with 22 pre-specified variables, risk of bias assessment using Cochrane RoB 2.0, primary outcome (12-month relapse rate as binary outcome, risk ratio as effect measure), statistical plan (random-effects DerSimonian-Laird model, I² and τ² heterogeneity, pre-specified subgroup analyses by number of prior episodes and concurrent medication use), and GRADE evidence certainty assessment.","tags":["literature-review","systematic-review","PRISMA","meta-analysis","mental-health"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:40:00Z","featured":true},

  {"id":"sci-22","title":"Narrative Literature Review: Structuring Evidence Hierarchies","prompt":"You are a senior academic in nutritional epidemiology commissioned to write a 4,000-word narrative review on the evidence for ultra-processed food (UPF) consumption and all-cause mortality for publication in the British Medical Journal. Produce a detailed section-by-section outline that: introduces the NOVA food classification system and its operationalisation in cohort studies, critically evaluates the hierarchy of evidence (RCT versus prospective cohort versus cross-sectional), summarises the five largest prospective cohort studies (NutriNet-Santé, UK Biobank, PREDIMED-Plus, NIH-AARP, Sun et al. 2019) including their design strengths, sample sizes, follow-up duration, and HR estimates for all-cause mortality, identifies three major methodological limitations common to all observational UPF studies (dietary assessment error, confounding by socioeconomic status, residual confounding by overall dietary quality), evaluates proposed biological mechanisms (gut microbiome dysbiosis, food additive direct toxicity, neuroendocrine appetite dysregulation), and concludes with a graded summary of evidence using the Bradford Hill criteria. Include a table structure for the study comparison and a suggested GRADE evidence certainty rating.","tags":["literature-review","narrative-review","ultra-processed-food","epidemiology","BMJ"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:45:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-23","title":"Scoping Review Methodology: Mapping a New Field","prompt":"You are a researcher designing a scoping review following Arksey and O'Malley's framework (updated by Levac et al. 2010) to map the breadth of evidence on the use of large language models (LLMs) in clinical decision support systems published between January 2020 and December 2024. The review is not intended to synthesise effect sizes but to characterise the landscape of research. Specify: the research question using the PCC framework (Population: clinical settings; Concept: LLM-based decision support; Context: any healthcare system), the database search strategy (PubMed, IEEE Xplore, ACM Digital Library, Embase, Google Scholar for grey literature), inclusion/exclusion criteria (include: empirical studies, implementation reports, systematic evaluations; exclude: opinion pieces, editorials, non-LLM AI systems), the charting form with 15 pre-specified variables (clinical domain, LLM architecture, evaluation metric, patient safety outcomes, validation method), how results will be presented as a bubble chart mapping clinical domains versus performance metrics, the distinction between scoping review and systematic review in terms of quality appraisal (scoping reviews do not formally appraise study quality), and how PRISMA-ScR 2018 reporting guidelines will be applied.","tags":["literature-review","scoping-review","LLM","clinical-decision-support","evidence-mapping"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:50:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-24","title":"Critical Appraisal of an RCT Using CONSORT","prompt":"You are a journal club facilitator for a cardiology research group. [Paste the paper or literature excerpt to critique]\n\nApply a structured CONSORT-based critical appraisal to this randomised controlled trial. Evaluate the following domains and assign a quality rating (adequate/inadequate/unclear) with a one-sentence justification for each: sequence generation and allocation concealment (was the randomisation truly concealed from clinicians enrolling patients?), blinding (of participants, care providers, outcome assessors — identify which were blinded and assess whether unblinding was plausible given the intervention), attrition bias (report the dropout rates per arm, test whether missingness is differential, evaluate the appropriateness of ITT versus per-protocol analysis), selective outcome reporting (compare the trial registration endpoints on ClinicalTrials.gov with the published primary outcome), baseline imbalance (identify any clinically meaningful imbalances in Table 1 that may act as confounders), and statistical analysis (was the pre-specified primary analysis followed, were all reported p-values two-tailed, was a correction applied for multiple comparisons?). Conclude with an overall risk-of-bias rating and a single sentence on whether the conclusions are justified by the data.","tags":["literature-review","critical-appraisal","CONSORT","RCT","journal-club"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:55:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-25","title":"Meta-Analysis: Interpreting Heterogeneity and Publication Bias","prompt":"You are a biostatistician co-authoring a meta-analysis of 22 studies examining the effect of aerobic exercise on fasting plasma glucose in adults with prediabetes. The pooled result shows a mean reduction of -0.41 mmol/L (95% CI -0.58 to -0.24), but I² = 74% indicating substantial heterogeneity. Walk through the complete analytical response to this level of heterogeneity: (1) confirm that a random-effects model (DerSimonian-Laird) rather than fixed-effects is appropriate and explain why; (2) interpret τ² = 0.089 in practical terms; (3) conduct a pre-specified subgroup analysis by exercise modality (HIIT n=8 studies vs moderate-intensity continuous n=14 studies) — explain how to test whether the subgroup difference itself is statistically significant using a chi-squared test for interaction; (4) run a meta-regression in R using the metafor package with duration of intervention (weeks) and mean baseline fasting glucose as continuous moderators; (5) assess publication bias using Egger's test and a trim-and-fill analysis — explain how to report and interpret a funnel plot asymmetry in the context of heterogeneity. Write results sentences suitable for the manuscript.","tags":["literature-review","meta-analysis","heterogeneity","publication-bias","biostatistics"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-26","title":"Living Systematic Review Design","prompt":"You are a systematic review methodologist advising a team at the WHO Evidence-to-Policy Unit who want to maintain a living systematic review on mRNA vaccine safety in immunocompromised adults, updated monthly as new evidence emerges from ClinicalTrials.gov, VAERS, EudraVigilance, and published literature. Design the complete infrastructure for a living review that must be sustainable with a team of four reviewers working part-time. Specify: the software stack (Cochrane's RobotSearch for automated search, Rayyan for screening, Covidence for extraction, R with meta package for analysis, and GitHub for version control), the trigger criteria for updating the pooled estimate (when one or more new RCTs with n>200 are identified), the search automation schedule (weekly PubMed and EMBASE alerts via EPPI-Reviewer 4), how to manage cumulative type I error inflation across monthly updates using a sequential meta-analysis approach with an alpha-spending function (O'Brien-Fleming boundary), the governance structure for authorship on living documents, and the version control system for the PROSPERO registration. Explain how this design differs from a traditional Cochrane Review.","tags":["literature-review","living-systematic-review","WHO","vaccine-safety","evidence-synthesis"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:05:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-27","title":"Evidence Gap Map Construction","prompt":"You are a knowledge synthesis specialist designing an evidence gap map (EGM) to visualise the state of evidence on digital health interventions for secondary prevention of cardiovascular disease for a report to the National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE). Describe the full construction process: (1) define the rows of the EGM (interventions: mobile apps, wearables, telemedicine, remote monitoring, digital therapeutics) and columns (outcomes: blood pressure, LDL cholesterol, medication adherence, physical activity, 30-day hospital readmission, quality of life, mortality) based on a stakeholder prioritisation exercise using a modified Delphi with 15 cardiologists and patient representatives; (2) explain the systematic search strategy yielding 342 studies after deduplication; (3) describe how studies are placed in cells by intervention-outcome combination with bubble size indicating study volume and bubble shading indicating overall evidence quality (GRADE); (4) identify where the map shows the three largest evidence gaps (wearables for mortality, digital therapeutics for medication adherence, telemedicine for LDL in BAME populations); (5) specify the EGMap software (EPPI-Mapper) and the stakeholder dissemination plan. Approximately 500 words.","tags":["literature-review","evidence-gap-map","NICE","cardiovascular","digital-health"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:10:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-28","title":"Rapid Evidence Assessment for Policy","prompt":"You are a senior researcher at a government science advisory body producing a Rapid Evidence Assessment (REA) on the effectiveness of sugar-sweetened beverage (SSB) taxes in reducing consumption and improving health outcomes, commissioned for a Treasury policy brief due in 10 working days. Describe the abbreviated methodology compared to a full systematic review: compressed database search (PubMed, Cochrane, Campbell Collaboration, one grey literature source: NBER working papers), time-bound search (2010-2024 only), no formal GRADE assessment but a narrative quality summary, inclusion of natural experiments and interrupted time series studies alongside RCTs. Produce a structured template for the brief including: policy question in PICO format, a one-table evidence summary of the 8 highest-quality studies with design, country, tax rate, and effect size on consumption (%), key findings narrative (250 words maximum), knowledge gaps and applicability limitations to UK context (population size, health inequality effects, industry reformulation effects), and a confidence rating for each recommendation (high/moderate/low/very low). Note the distinction between REA and full systematic review for the policy audience.","tags":["literature-review","rapid-evidence-assessment","policy","sugar-tax","public-health"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:15:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-29","title":"Umbrella Review of Systematic Reviews","prompt":"You are an evidence synthesis expert writing the methods section of an umbrella review examining the association between shift work and metabolic syndrome across existing systematic reviews and meta-analyses. Explain what an umbrella review is and how it differs from a systematic review of primary studies. Write the Methods section (450 words) covering: eligibility criteria (only systematic reviews and meta-analyses of observational studies, minimum 5 primary studies per review, published 2010-2024), search strategy (PubMed, EMBASE, PsycINFO with MeSH terms: 'shift work OR night shift' AND 'metabolic syndrome OR insulin resistance OR obesity'), quality assessment of included reviews using AMSTAR-2 (16-domain tool), the challenge of primary study overlap (calculating the corrected covered area, CCA, using the overlap matrix), how to present overlapping findings without double-counting primary studies, the pooling approach (when to pool across reviews and when to present narratively given AMSTAR-2 critically low ratings), and how the GRADE framework applies when rating evidence from an umbrella review rather than primary studies. Provide an example table structure for the evidence overview.","tags":["literature-review","umbrella-review","shift-work","metabolic-syndrome","AMSTAR-2"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:20:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-30","title":"Citation Management and Reference Audit","prompt":"You are a scientific writing advisor helping a PhD student finalise the reference list of her doctoral thesis on epigenetic modifications in early embryo development. She has 248 references managed in Zotero and suspects several have formatting errors, incorrect authors from PubMed auto-import issues, and retracted papers she has not identified. Walk through a systematic reference audit process: (1) how to run a retraction check against the Retraction Watch database and CrossRef's retraction flag API for all 248 DOIs using a Python script with the requests library and pandas; (2) how to identify and fix common Zotero auto-import errors (corporate authors misformatted, preprint versions cited instead of final published versions, missing volume/issue for Nature family journals); (3) how to verify that every reference cited in the text appears in the reference list and vice versa using a Python script parsing the .docx body and Zotero BibTeX export; (4) how to apply the journal style (Cell Press author-date or numbered Vancouver) automatically using Zotero's CSL processor; and (5) how to generate a DOI-linked reference list for the digital submission. Provide the Python pseudocode for steps 1 and 3.","tags":["literature-review","citation-management","Zotero","reference-audit","Retraction-Watch"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:25:00Z","featured":false},

  // --- 4. GRANT WRITING & FUNDING (sci-31 to sci-40) ---
  {"id":"sci-31","title":"NIH R01 Specific Aims Page: Neurodegeneration","prompt":"You are a senior neuroscientist and experienced NIH grant writer with 20 years of R01 funding history. Write a compelling one-page Specific Aims section for an NIH R01 application to NINDS investigating whether astrocyte-derived lipocalin-2 (LCN2) drives neuroinflammation-mediated synapse loss in early Parkinson's disease. The page must follow the canonical structure: opening paragraph (4 sentences establishing clinical urgency — 1M US PD patients, lack of disease-modifying therapy, the synaptic loss hypothesis preceding dopaminergic cell death) and a clear knowledge gap sentence; central hypothesis (LCN2 secreted by reactive astrocytes binds TLR4 on dopaminergic neurons, activating NF-κB and initiating complement-mediated synapse elimination); three specific aims with bold titles (Aim 1: Characterise LCN2 expression and TLR4 co-localisation in human substantia nigra using post-mortem tissue from the UK Brain Banks; Aim 2: Test whether LCN2 knockout or anti-LCN2 nanobody treatment rescues synaptic density and motor function in AAV-α-synuclein mice; Aim 3: Identify the LCN2-induced transcriptional programme in primary dopaminergic neurons by ATAC-seq and RNA-seq); expected outcome per aim; closing innovation and impact statement. 420-450 words.","tags":["grant-writing","NIH-R01","neuroscience","Parkinson","neuroinflammation"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:30:00Z","featured":true},

  {"id":"sci-32","title":"NSF CAREER Award: Broader Impacts Section","prompt":"You are an assistant professor of computational chemistry at a state university preparing your NSF CAREER award application. Write the Broader Impacts section (750 words) for a project developing machine-learning force fields for predicting protein-lipid membrane interactions relevant to drug delivery. The Broader Impacts must go beyond research training and address NSF's five criteria: (1) advance discovery while promoting training — describe your research group's planned structure (2 PhD students, 1 postdoc, annual REU participant from an HBCU partner institution), (2) broaden participation — detail your partnership with Xavier University of Louisiana (HBCU), your role teaching a two-week computational chemistry bootcamp for undergraduate students from groups underrepresented in STEM, and your mentorship framework using Slack-based weekly check-ins and IDP planning; (3) enhance infrastructure — describe the open-source Python package (MLMembrane) you will release on GitHub with documentation and tutorial Jupyter notebooks; (4) broad dissemination — detail your plan for preprints on ChemRxiv, data deposition on Zenodo, and one public-facing video explainer per paper posted on YouTube; (5) societal benefit — explain how improved membrane interaction models accelerate lipid nanoparticle design for mRNA therapeutics. Use NSF's preferred active voice and first-person plural.","tags":["grant-writing","NSF-CAREER","broader-impacts","computational-chemistry","HBCU"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:35:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-33","title":"NIH Biosketch: Personal Statement and Contributions","prompt":"You are a research administrator helping a mid-career immunologist prepare a 2024-format NIH Biosketch for inclusion in a multi-PI R01 application. Write the Personal Statement section (250-350 words) for Dr. Miriam Santos, an associate professor of immunology with expertise in regulatory T cell (Treg) biology and mucosal immunity, who will serve as Co-Investigator contributing Treg functional assays and the humanised mouse model component. The Personal Statement must: establish her specific relevance to this grant (not a general career summary), highlight three publications directly relevant to the proposed work (a 2021 JCI paper characterising iTreg induction by butyrate, a 2022 Nature Immunology paper on Foxp3 stability in colitis, and a 2023 Cell Host & Microbe paper using humanised mice for gut microbiome studies), explain her unique technical capabilities (HIS mouse colony maintenance, lamina propria Treg isolation by FACS, and Treg suppression assays), and describe her collaborative relationship with the PI (six years of collaboration, two shared publications, complementary expertise). Then write two Contributions to Science entries in the required format with up to four citations each.","tags":["grant-writing","NIH-biosketch","immunology","regulatory-T-cells","mucosal-immunity"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:40:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-34","title":"MRC Confidence in Concept: Budget Justification","prompt":"You are a grant writer helping a junior researcher prepare a Medical Research Council (MRC) Confidence in Concept (CiC) application for £50,000 to fund a 12-month feasibility study testing whether a CRISPR base-editing approach can correct the Gly380Arg FGFR3 mutation causing achondroplasia in patient-derived iPSCs. Write the complete Budget Justification narrative (400 words) covering: salary costs (0.2 FTE postdoc at Grade 7 spine point 29, £9,840 including 28% on-costs), consumables (iPSC culture reagents £12,000, base-editing reagents and DNA sequencing £8,500, protein analysis reagents £4,200), capital equipment (no new equipment — justify use of existing departmental electroporators and confocal microscope with full cost recovery rates), training (£1,500 for postdoc attending EMBL base editing practical course), overheads (at 46.5% fEC, indirect costs = £16,183, but MRC funds 80% = £12,946), and a contingency paragraph explaining the 5% contingency line (£1,714) is for cell line authentication costs if reprogramming requires a second iPSC line. Justify every line with a scientific rationale and vendor quotation reference. Format as the MRC Je-S system requires.","tags":["grant-writing","MRC","budget-justification","CRISPR","iPSC"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:45:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-35","title":"ERC Starting Grant: State of the Art and Objectives","prompt":"You are an experienced ERC grant writer advising an early-career PI preparing an ERC Starting Grant application (StG) in the domain of structural biology. The project proposes using cryo-electron tomography (cryo-ET) combined with AI-assisted subtomogram averaging to determine the in situ structure of the bacterial type VI secretion system (T6SS) during active firing in intact Pseudomonas aeruginosa cells. Write the State of the Art and Objectives section (800 words) following the ERC StG guidelines: summarise the current knowledge of T6SS structure from purified systems (Mougous lab Sci Adv 2023 model), identify the key gap (lack of in situ structures during active firing with millisecond time resolution), explain the technical breakthrough that now makes this feasible (new Volta phase plate cryo-ET at 100 kV, AMIRA-AI subtomogram averaging reducing computational time by 10-fold, the team's published correlative light and electron microscopy (CLEM) pipeline), state five specific scientific objectives as numbered measurable goals, and argue why this project is high-risk/high-reward (ERC's primary selection criterion) because cryo-ET of active membrane complexes has a 40% failure rate but would transform structural understanding of bacterial secretion. Use concise, confident, non-jargon language appropriate for a multidisciplinary ERC panel.","tags":["grant-writing","ERC","structural-biology","cryo-ET","T6SS"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:50:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-36","title":"Resubmission Introduction (NIH A1): Addressing Reviewer Critique","prompt":"You are an NIH-funded principal investigator resubmitting a revised R01 application (A1) to NIGMS that was scored but not funded in the previous cycle (impact score 35, percentile 22nd). Reviewers raised three concerns: (1) the proposed mouse model of non-alcoholic steatohepatitis (NASH) using high-fat/high-fructose diet is not translationally relevant because it lacks the fibrosis seen in human NASH; (2) the transcriptomic approach lacks spatial resolution; (3) the PI's lab has no published experience in hepatic fibrosis. Write the mandatory one-page Introduction to the Revised Application that: acknowledges each criticism respectfully without being defensive, summarises the responsive changes (switched to the GAN diet model which achieves NASH with stage 2-3 fibrosis within 16 weeks; added 10x Genomics Visium spatial transcriptomics to Aim 2; recruited Dr. Ramon Garcia at UCSF as a co-investigator who is a hepatic fibrosis expert with 12 published papers in the field), and closes with a statement of increased confidence in the revised approach. Must not exceed one page single-spaced. Tone: confident, collegial, scientifically rigorous.","tags":["grant-writing","NIH-A1","resubmission","NASH","reviewer-response"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:55:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-37","title":"Wellcome Trust Discovery Award: Significance Section","prompt":"You are a grant writing specialist helping a team of three investigators apply for a Wellcome Trust Discovery Award (formerly Senior Investigator Award) for a £2.5M 5-year project investigating the evolutionary origins of vertebrate adaptive immunity by reconstructing ancestral jawless fish (lamprey) immune gene circuits using synthetic biology. Write the Scientific Significance section (600 words) following Wellcome's 2024 application guidance which emphasises transformative potential, interdisciplinary novelty, and long-term impact. The section must argue that: the evolutionary transition from innate-only to adaptive immunity remains the most significant unresolved question in immunology; existing lamprey immune studies are limited to gene expression correlation (cite Pancer 2004 Science, Herrin 2008 PNAS); the synthetic reconstruction approach — building ancestral VLR (variable lymphocyte receptor) gene circuits in human cell lines using Gibson assembly and dCas9-based transcriptional circuits — is genuinely transformative because it moves from correlation to mechanism; success would overturn current models of RAG transposon co-option in adaptive immunity evolution. Explain why Wellcome (rather than an MRC or BBSRC grant) is the appropriate funder, referencing Wellcome's stated priority for 'blue-skies discovery science'.","tags":["grant-writing","Wellcome-Trust","immunology","evolution","synthetic-biology"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-38","title":"Industry-Funded Research Contract: Scientific Deliverables Section","prompt":"You are a research contracts officer at a university technology transfer office helping a chemistry department negotiate a sponsored research agreement with a pharmaceutical company for a 2-year, £320,000 project developing novel HDAC6-selective inhibitors for Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease. Draft the Scientific Deliverables and Milestones section of the research contract, specifying: six-monthly milestones with clear go/no-go criteria (Month 6: deliver 20 compounds with IC50 <100 nM against HDAC6 and >10-fold selectivity over HDAC1 by fluorescence-based assay; Month 12: deliver 5 lead compounds with acceptable permeability in PAMPA assay, Papp >5×10⁻⁶ cm/s; Month 18: demonstrate efficacy in the CMT2F mouse model by grip strength and nerve conduction velocity with p<0.05; Month 24: full candidate report). Include an Intellectual Property clause template specifying that foreground IP generated by university staff belongs to the university but the company holds a non-exclusive licence for the specific disease indication. Specify publication rights (12-month delay for patent filing, then free to publish), data ownership, and the right of the university to use results for academic purposes. Format as a contract schedule.","tags":["grant-writing","industry-research","sponsored-research-agreement","HDAC6","IP"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:05:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-39","title":"Fellowship Personal Statement: Marie Curie Postdoctoral Fellowship","prompt":"You are a career development advisor helping a postdoctoral researcher in computational neuroscience write the Excellence section of a Marie Skłodowska-Curie Postdoctoral Fellowship (MSCA-PF) application. The applicant, Dr. Yuki Tanaka, proposes to move from RIKEN (Japan) to the Sainsbury Wellcome Centre (UK) to develop a Bayesian network model of hippocampal-prefrontal cortex communication during spatial working memory, combining her existing expertise in calcium imaging data analysis with the host lab's two-photon mesoscope platform and Neuropixels recording capabilities. Write the Researcher Profile section (500 words) that: establishes her track record (3 first-author papers including one in Nature Neuroscience 2023), articulates why this specific trans-national move creates scientific synergy unavailable in either location alone, describes the novel technical skills she will acquire (large-scale multi-region Neuropixels recordings and the host lab's proprietary pynapple Python toolbox for neural data analysis), and explains the career development dimension (establishing an independent research group in Europe, acquiring open-source software development skills). Write in first person, following Marie Curie guidance for 'Excellence' criterion B1.1.","tags":["grant-writing","MSCA","fellowship","computational-neuroscience","career-development"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:10:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-40","title":"Data Management Plan for a Multi-Centre Clinical Study","prompt":"You are a research data manager helping the chief investigator of a 14-centre UK clinical trial (IRAS-approved, n=620 participants with chronic kidney disease) write a Data Management Plan (DMP) compliant with the UK Research Integrity Office guidelines and required by NIHR for all HTA Programme grants. Write the full DMP (600 words) covering: data types generated (REDCap eCRF data for 48 variables per visit × 4 visits, DEXA scan DICOM files, spot urine biobanked samples with sample tracking in Freezerworks), data governance (GDPR Article 9 special category health data, pseudonymisation protocol, IG Toolkit compliance with NHS DSP Toolkit, Data Processing Agreement between sponsor and sites), storage and security (primary storage on NHS N3 encrypted server at coordinating centre with daily backup to university secure cloud, access control by role with audit trail in REDCap), data sharing plan (ISRCTN registration with results reporting within 12 months of trial end, anonymised participant-level dataset deposited in the UK Data Service with 2-year embargo, availability as restricted access with data sharing agreement), and data preservation (minimum 15-year retention per MHRA clinical trials regulations, archiving to ISO-certified repository). Include a sentence on what happens to data if the PI moves institutions.","tags":["grant-writing","data-management","DMP","clinical-trial","GDPR"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:15:00Z","featured":false},

  // --- 5. DATA ANALYSIS & STATISTICS (sci-41 to sci-50) ---
  {"id":"sci-41","title":"Mixed-Effects Model for Longitudinal Clinical Data in R","prompt":"You are a biostatistician consulting on a longitudinal study measuring C-reactive protein (CRP, mg/L, log-transformed) at baseline and 4, 8, 12, and 24 weeks in 180 participants randomised 1:1 to a dietary intervention versus control. There is 22% missing data at 24 weeks. Write a complete R analysis script using the nlme and lme4 packages to fit a linear mixed-effects model (MMRM: mixed model for repeated measures) with the following specification: fixed effects (treatment arm, visit, treatment×visit interaction, baseline CRP as covariate, age and sex as adjustment covariates), random effects (random intercept per participant), and an unstructured covariance matrix for the residuals. Include code for: data reshaping from wide to long format using tidyr::pivot_longer, checking the normality of residuals with a QQ-plot and Shapiro-Wilk test, checking the homoscedasticity assumption with a Breusch-Pagan test, extracting the treatment effect at week 24 with 95% CI and p-value using emmeans with Kenward-Roger degrees of freedom, producing a spaghetti plot of individual trajectories coloured by arm using ggplot2, and interpreting the interaction term. State which packages and their versions were used for reproducibility.","tags":["data-analysis","R","mixed-effects-model","longitudinal","clinical-statistics"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:20:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-42","title":"Bayesian Analysis: Interpreting Prior Sensitivity","prompt":"You are a Bayesian statistician advising a team that has completed a small Phase II trial (n=45 per arm) of a gene therapy for spinal muscular atrophy, measuring six-minute walk distance improvement at 52 weeks. The trial pre-specified a Bayesian analysis using a normal prior for the treatment effect centred on the previous natural history study (mean = +8 metres, SD = 15 metres). The observed data yield a likelihood centred on +22 metres (SD = 18 metres). Walk through the complete Bayesian update: (1) write out Bayes' theorem in the conjugate normal-normal case and calculate the posterior mean and posterior SD analytically; (2) explain what the posterior 95% credible interval means versus a frequentist 95% confidence interval — specifically address the common misconception that a CI has 95% probability of containing the true value; (3) conduct a prior sensitivity analysis testing three alternative priors (weakly informative: mean=0, SD=30; sceptical: mean=0, SD=10; enthusiastic: mean=20, SD=10) and show how the posterior changes under each in a table; (4) define the Region of Practical Equivalence (ROPE) as [-5, +5] metres and calculate the probability that the treatment effect falls outside the ROPE under each prior. Use R code with the bayestestR package.","tags":["data-analysis","Bayesian","prior-sensitivity","credible-interval","clinical-trial"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:25:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-43","title":"Multiple Comparison Correction: When and Why","prompt":"You are a statistical reviewer at a neuroscience journal. A paper reports 24 independent t-tests across 24 brain regions comparing grey matter volume between patients with treatment-resistant depression (n=38) and healthy controls (n=40) using voxel-based morphometry from 3T MRI data. The paper reports 6 significant results at p < 0.05 uncorrected and claims these represent genuine findings. Write a technical critique (500 words) that: explains the family-wise error rate (FWER) calculation for 24 tests at alpha=0.05 (probability of at least one false positive = 1-(0.95)²⁴ = 71%), distinguishes between FWER-controlling methods (Bonferroni, Holm-Bonferroni) and FDR-controlling methods (Benjamini-Hochberg, Storey's q-value) and explains which is appropriate for exploratory neuroimaging, applies Bonferroni correction (α_corrected = 0.05/24 = 0.0021) and states which of the 6 reported findings survive (assuming the reported p-values range from 0.002 to 0.048), explains why cluster-based permutation testing using FSL randomise is the field-standard for VBM analyses, and recommends the specific analytical changes required before the paper can be reconsidered for publication. Tone: rigorous but constructive.","tags":["data-analysis","multiple-comparisons","FWER","FDR","neuroimaging"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:30:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-44","title":"Survival Analysis: Kaplan-Meier and Cox Regression","prompt":"You are a biostatistician writing the Statistical Analysis section and Results for a paper examining time to COPD exacerbation requiring hospitalisation in 320 patients with GOLD stage III-IV COPD followed for up to 3 years. The primary analysis is a Kaplan-Meier survival analysis comparing patients treated with triple inhaler therapy (ICS/LABA/LAMA, n=160) versus LABA/LAMA alone (n=160), and a multivariable Cox proportional hazards regression adjusting for FEV1% predicted, smoking pack-years, prior exacerbation rate, and eosinophil count. Write: (1) the Statistical Analysis Methods paragraph specifying log-rank test, median survival times with 95% CI by Brookmeyer-Crowley method, and Cox model assumptions testing (Schoenfeld residuals test for proportional hazards, dfbeta plots for influential observations); (2) the Results paragraph with the Kaplan-Meier result (median time to exacerbation: triple therapy 18.4 months [95%CI 15.2-22.1] vs LABA/LAMA 11.7 months [95%CI 9.3-14.8], log-rank p=0.003); (3) the Cox regression results table with adjusted HR, 95% CI, and p-value for each covariate; (4) interpretation of the HR for triple therapy (aHR=0.62, 95%CI 0.47-0.82) in absolute risk terms using the baseline cumulative hazard at 12 months.","tags":["data-analysis","survival-analysis","Kaplan-Meier","Cox-regression","COPD"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:35:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-45","title":"Principal Component Analysis in Metabolomics","prompt":"You are a bioinformatician helping a metabolomics team analyse an untargeted LC-MS dataset of 1,240 metabolic features measured in serum samples from 80 patients with systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) and 80 age-and-sex-matched healthy controls, pre-processed using XCMS and normalised by probabilistic quotient normalisation (PQN). Walk through the complete multivariate analysis pipeline in R using the ropls package: (1) perform and interpret an unsupervised PCA — explain how to read the scores plot (PC1 vs PC2), what clustering indicates, and how to identify batch effects or outliers using Hotelling's T² ellipse; (2) explain why the first step must always be unsupervised PCA before supervised methods; (3) perform a supervised Partial Least Squares-Discriminant Analysis (PLS-DA) with 7-fold cross-validation — interpret the R²X, R²Y, and Q² values and explain the Q²>0.5 threshold for a model considered predictive; (4) extract the Variable Importance in Projection (VIP) scores and identify features with VIP>1.0 as the primary feature selection criterion; (5) describe how to validate the PLS-DA model using 1000-iteration permutation testing and interpret the permuted Q² distribution. Produce annotated R code for steps 1-5.","tags":["data-analysis","PCA","PLS-DA","metabolomics","R"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:40:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-46","title":"Effect Size Reporting and Clinical Significance","prompt":"You are a statistical methodology expert writing a tutorial for clinical researchers on the distinction between statistical significance and clinical significance. Using a worked example from a hypertension RCT (n=4,200 per arm, mean systolic BP reduction: treatment -3.1 mmHg vs control -0.8 mmHg, pooled SD=18 mmHg, p < 0.0001), demonstrate: (1) why the p-value is extremely small despite a clinically modest effect, explained by the relationship between p-value, effect size, and sample size (the formula t = d × √(n/2)); (2) calculation and interpretation of Cohen's d = 0.13 (negligible by conventional benchmarks), NNT for a cardiovascular event (assuming 10% 5-year event rate reduction of 12% → NNT=83), and absolute risk reduction (1.2%); (3) the minimal clinically important difference (MCID) concept — explain why a 3 mmHg BP reduction falls below the commonly accepted 5 mmHg MCID for this indication; (4) how to communicate these findings to a non-statistical clinical audience without distorting the evidence; (5) the proper way to report effect sizes in accordance with APA 7th edition Publication Manual and EQUATOR reporting guidelines. Approximately 550 words.","tags":["data-analysis","effect-size","clinical-significance","NNT","statistics"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:45:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-47","title":"RNA-Seq Differential Expression Analysis in DESeq2","prompt":"You are a bioinformatician writing the statistical analysis section for an RNA-seq study comparing transcriptomic profiles of pancreatic stellate cells (PSCs) activated by TGF-β1 (10 ng/mL, 48h) versus quiescent PSCs, with n=4 biological replicates per condition, sequenced on Illumina NovaSeq 6000 with 2×150 bp paired-end reads at 40M reads per sample. Write a complete Methods paragraph and annotated R code for the DESeq2 analysis pipeline: raw count matrix import from featureCounts output, DESeqDataSetFromMatrix construction with the ~condition design formula, size factor estimation by median-of-ratios normalisation, negative binomial dispersion modelling with MAP shrinkage, Wald test for differential expression, independent filtering at baseMean >10, Benjamini-Hochberg FDR correction with adjusted p-value threshold of 0.05 and log2FoldChange threshold of 1.0, apeglm log fold-change shrinkage for accurate volcano plots, and gene set enrichment analysis (GSEA) using the fgsea package with MSigDB Hallmark gene sets. Describe how to interpret the dispersion-mean plot to assess data quality and what an overdispersed outlier indicates. Provide the complete code from count matrix to a publication-quality MA plot using ggplot2.","tags":["data-analysis","RNA-seq","DESeq2","differential-expression","bioinformatics"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:50:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-48","title":"Machine Learning in Biomedical Research: Avoiding Overfitting","prompt":"You are a computational biologist advising a PhD student who has built a random forest classifier in scikit-learn to predict treatment response (responder/non-responder) from 847 plasma protein features measured at baseline in 120 cancer patients. The model achieves 91% accuracy on the training set and 61% on a held-out test set (20% split). Write a structured critique and remediation guide (550 words): (1) diagnose the problem — overfitting due to high-dimensional features (p=847) relative to sample size (n=96 training), the curse of dimensionality, and data leakage if feature selection was performed before splitting; (2) recommend a nested cross-validation framework (outer 5-fold CV for performance estimation, inner 5-fold CV for hyperparameter tuning using RandomizedSearchCV) and explain why a single train-test split gives a biased performance estimate with n=120; (3) recommend dimensionality reduction before modelling — LASSO logistic regression (L1 penalty with lambda selected by inner CV) to reduce features to <30; (4) specify the correct performance metrics for an imbalanced dataset (responders 35%, non-responders 65%): area under the precision-recall curve (AUPRC) preferred over AUC-ROC, F1 score, and MCC (Matthews Correlation Coefficient); (5) describe the permutation test for model significance (shuffle labels 1000×, compare null AUC distribution to observed AUC). Provide the corrected scikit-learn pipeline as pseudocode.","tags":["data-analysis","machine-learning","overfitting","scikit-learn","biomedical"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:55:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-49","title":"Mediation Analysis in Epidemiology","prompt":"You are an epidemiological statistician helping a team investigate whether the association between socioeconomic deprivation (exposure, measured by Index of Multiple Deprivation decile) and cardiovascular disease incidence (outcome, binary, 10-year follow-up) in 8,540 adults from the EPIC-Norfolk cohort is mediated by physical inactivity (mediator, accelerometer-derived moderate-to-vigorous physical activity minutes/week). Describe the counterfactual framework for causal mediation analysis and explain why traditional Baron and Kenny steps are insufficient for binary outcomes. Write the statistical analysis plan using the R mediation package or PROC CAUSALMED in SAS: (1) specify the outcome model (logistic regression: CVD ~ deprivation + MVPA + age + sex + smoking + BMI) and the mediator model (linear regression: MVPA ~ deprivation + age + sex); (2) define the natural direct effect (NDE), natural indirect effect (NIE), and total effect using the potential outcomes framework; (3) estimate the proportion mediated as NIE/total effect on the log-odds ratio scale; (4) use bootstrapping (1000 samples) to obtain 95% CIs for all mediated effects; (5) describe the four key assumptions required for valid causal mediation analysis (no unmeasured exposure-outcome confounding, no unmeasured mediator-outcome confounding, no unmeasured exposure-mediator confounding, no mediator-outcome confounder affected by the exposure) and evaluate their plausibility in this study.","tags":["data-analysis","mediation-analysis","epidemiology","causal-inference","R"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-50","title":"Data Visualisation for High-Dimensional Genomics Data","prompt":"You are a bioinformatics data visualisation expert helping a research group create a publication-quality multi-panel figure for a Cell paper describing single-cell multi-omics (simultaneous scRNA-seq and scATAC-seq using 10x Genomics Multiome) of human bone marrow from five healthy donors. Design the complete figure architecture for a six-panel figure using R (Seurat 5, Signac, ggplot2, patchwork): Panel A (UMAP of 28,000 cells coloured by cell type, 14 annotated populations, point size 0.2, alpha 0.6, legend with abbreviated labels); Panel B (dot plot of top 3 marker genes per cell type, dot size = fraction expressing, colour = scaled average expression, colour scale: Viridis Magma); Panel C (ATAC-seq genome browser track for the GATA1 locus in three cell populations using Signac's CoveragePlot with gene annotation track and ChIP-seq peak annotations); Panel D (Sankey diagram showing chromatin accessibility state transitions from CMP to MEP to erythroid progenitors using ggalluvial); Panel E (volcano plot of differentially accessible peaks in HSCs versus MPPs, ggplot2, label top 10 peaks by -log10 adjusted p-value); Panel F (trajectory pseudotime analysis using Monocle3, three lineages shown as coloured paths on UMAP). Specify exact colour palettes, axis labelling standards, font sizes (8pt minimum for print), and figure dimensions for submission at 300 DPI.","tags":["data-analysis","visualisation","single-cell","UMAP","genomics"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:05:00Z","featured":false},


  // --- 6. PEER REVIEW & CRITIQUE (sci-51 to sci-60) ---
  {"id":"sci-51","title":"Writing a Rigorous Peer Review for Nature Biotechnology","prompt":"You are an expert reviewer for Nature Biotechnology with 15 years of experience evaluating manuscripts on gene therapy, synthetic biology, and molecular diagnostics. [Paste the paper or literature excerpt to critique]\n\nWrite a complete peer review report for this manuscript structured as follows: (1) Summary paragraph (100 words): summarise the study's central claim, approach, and significance in your own words — this proves you read it; (2) Major Concerns (3-5 points): for each, identify the specific claim, the experiment or data that fails to support it, and describe the exact additional experiment or analysis needed to resolve the concern (be precise — 'provide western blots for n=3 biological replicates' not 'strengthen the data'); (3) Minor Concerns (3-5 points): nomenclature inconsistencies, missing statistical details, mislabelled figure panels, incomplete Methods; (4) Recommendation: Accept, Minor Revision, Major Revision, or Reject with a one-sentence justification. Do not make your recommendation in the first paragraph. Do not comment on writing quality unless it impedes comprehension of the science. Maintain a collegial, constructive tone — the goal is to improve the work.","tags":["peer-review","manuscript-review","Nature-Biotechnology","gene-therapy","critique"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:10:00Z","featured":true},

  {"id":"sci-52","title":"Point-by-Point Rebuttal to Major Revision Decision","prompt":"You are a corresponding author preparing a point-by-point rebuttal letter to accompany a major revision of a manuscript submitted to the Journal of Clinical Investigation. Reviewer 2 raised four concerns about your study on extracellular HMGB1 as a DAMPs signal in sepsis-induced cardiomyopathy: (1) the mouse CLP sepsis model is not clinically relevant; (2) the cardiac function data show only echocardiographic changes without invasive haemodynamic validation; (3) Figure 6 shows the HMGB1 neutralising antibody experiment but lacks appropriate isotype control; (4) the conclusion that HMGB1 is 'required' for cardiomyopathy overreaches the pharmacological inhibition data. Write the rebuttal letter with a formal header and four point-by-point responses that: open each response by quoting the reviewer concern verbatim in italics, acknowledge any valid criticism directly without being defensive, describe the new experiments performed in response (for concern 1: added the LPS endotoxaemia model with cardiac data, n=10; for concern 2: added pressure-volume loop haemodynamics at 24h; for concern 3: added rat IgG isotype data included as new Supplementary Figure S4; for concern 4: revised the language to 'sufficient to induce' rather than 'required'), and cross-reference new manuscript line numbers. Close with a summary of all changes. Professional tone.","tags":["peer-review","rebuttal-letter","JCI","sepsis","manuscript-revision"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:15:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-53","title":"Statistical Review of a Clinical Trial Manuscript","prompt":"You are a statistical reviewer for The Lancet assigned to critically evaluate the statistical methods and reporting of a submitted manuscript describing a Phase III RCT of a biologic therapy for moderate-to-severe ulcerative colitis. Write a structured statistical review covering: (1) primary analysis — was the pre-specified primary endpoint (clinical remission at week 52 by Mayo score) analysed by the stated method (logistic regression with multiple imputation for missing data under MAR assumption), and was the non-responder imputation sensitivity analysis also reported as required for regulatory submissions?; (2) multiple testing — the paper reports 14 secondary endpoints at nominal p<0.05 without declaring a hierarchical testing procedure in the protocol, enumerate the risk of spurious findings; (3) subgroup analyses — are the 8 reported subgroup analyses pre-specified or post-hoc, and does the paper test for interaction rather than reporting significance within subgroups?; (4) effect size reporting — is NNT reported alongside the OR, and is the absolute risk difference provided?; (5) missing data — what percentage of participants had missing primary outcome data and was the MAR assumption tested with a tipping-point analysis? Provide specific line-by-line recommendations for the statistical methods and results sections.","tags":["peer-review","statistical-review","Lancet","RCT","missing-data"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:20:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-54","title":"Critical Appraisal of a Diagnostic Accuracy Study (STARD)","prompt":"You are a clinical epidemiologist leading a journal club for radiology residents. Apply a STARD 2015-based critical appraisal to a study evaluating a new AI-based algorithm for detecting pulmonary embolism on CT pulmonary angiography (CTPA) images compared to expert radiologist consensus as the reference standard. Evaluate each of the following STARD domains and flag specific concerns where applicable: patient spectrum (were consecutive patients enrolled or was there cherry-picking of easy cases and definite non-PE cases that inflates performance?), reference standard (is expert radiologist consensus truly a gold standard, or is there verification bias in which only high-probability cases received follow-up imaging?), blinding (were the AI system and radiologists blinded to each other's results?), missing data (how were uninterpretable CTPA studies handled — were they excluded, and if so does this introduce partial verification bias?), reporting of uncertainty (are likelihood ratios and 95% CIs reported in addition to sensitivity and specificity?), and clinical utility (was a decision curve analysis performed to establish net benefit at clinically relevant threshold probabilities?). Conclude with an overall QUADAS-2 risk of bias rating across four domains.","tags":["peer-review","STARD","diagnostic-accuracy","radiology","QUADAS-2"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:25:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-55","title":"Responding to an Unfair Rejection: Editor Appeal Letter","prompt":"You are a corresponding author appealing an outright rejection from PNAS. The handling editor rejected the paper without sending it to review, citing 'insufficient broad significance for the PNAS readership.' Your paper reports the discovery of a new class of RNA-binding proteins that regulate phase separation in stress granules by a previously unknown mechanism involving liquid-liquid phase transition kinetics monitored by single-molecule FRET, with direct implications for ALS and FTD pathology. Write a professional appeal letter to the editor-in-chief (750 words maximum) that: opens with a respectful but direct statement of the appeal, quotes the rejection reason verbatim and addresses it systematically, argues for broad significance by citing three recent PNAS papers on RNA phase separation biology (placeholder: Smith 2023, Lee 2022, Wang 2023) that establish precedent for the field's relevance to the PNAS audience, clarifies that the submission encompasses three independent mechanistic findings each of which would constitute a standalone significant advance, offers to revise the abstract and significance statement to make the breadth of impact clearer, and requests reconsideration by an editor with expertise in RNA biology. Maintain a respectful tone throughout — do not be accusatory. Do not restate the science in excessive technical detail.","tags":["peer-review","rejection-appeal","PNAS","RNA-biology","phase-separation"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:30:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-56","title":"Post-Publication Peer Review: Writing a PubPeer Comment","prompt":"You are a research scientist writing a constructive post-publication peer review comment on PubPeer about a Cell paper claiming that a ketogenic diet reverses tumour growth in a KRAS-mutant colorectal cancer mouse model by depleting fructose-6-phosphate. Your concern relates to two specific issues: (1) the Western blot panels in Figures 2C and 3A appear to share identical background patterns suggestive of image duplication, which you have documented by image forensic analysis using FiJi/ImageJ (describe the specific features you observed — identical pixel clusters in the membrane background at coordinates approximately 120×80 pixels); (2) the statistical analysis reports a two-tailed t-test for survival data, which is inappropriate and should be a log-rank test, likely inflating the significance of the result. Write the PubPeer comment in a factual, evidence-based, non-accusatory style that: describes the image concerns objectively with reference to the specific figure panels, provides the alternative statistical test and explains why it is correct, avoids ascribing intent to the authors, requests the authors provide original uncropped blots, and notes that you have contacted the journal's data integrity team. Follow the PubPeer community guidelines for constructive commentary.","tags":["peer-review","post-publication","PubPeer","image-integrity","research-integrity"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:35:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-57","title":"Editorial Decision Letter: Major Revision with Specific Instructions","prompt":"You are a handling editor at eLife preparing an editorial decision letter for a manuscript on mitochondrial dysfunction in cardiac aging. The paper has received three reviews: Reviewer 1 recommends minor revision; Reviewer 2 recommends major revision due to concerns about the model system (isolated cardiomyocytes versus intact heart) and lack of in vivo validation; Reviewer 3 recommends rejection due to the relatively modest mechanistic novelty. You have decided to offer Major Revision with a 6-week timeline. Write the editorial decision letter that: synthesises the three reviewers' assessments without revealing individual reviewer identities, articulates the essential revisions (using 'essential' and 'optional' categories per eLife's format), specifies that the in vivo validation using aged mouse echocardiography and Seahorse XF Analyser in cardiac tissue homogenates is an essential experiment, frames the optional experiments as opportunities to strengthen but not requirements, sets clear expectations for the revised manuscript (a detailed response letter addressing each point, revised figures with individual data points plotted for all n<20 datasets, updated statistical reporting per eLife's statistical checklist), and informs the authors that you will seek re-review from Reviewer 2 only. Professional, encouraging but direct tone.","tags":["peer-review","editorial-decision","eLife","cardiac-aging","mitochondria"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:40:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-58","title":"Grant Peer Review: Strengths and Weaknesses Scoring","prompt":"You are an NIH study section reviewer assigned to evaluate a new R01 application to NIDDK proposing to investigate the role of intestinal farnesoid X receptor (FXR) in regulating the gut-liver bile acid axis in non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD). Write a complete summary statement (pink sheet) structured according to NIH format: an overall impact score paragraph (1-9 scale, justify a score of 3 — high priority), then a criterion-by-criterion assessment of: Significance (what is the knowledge gap, is it important, what happens if the aims succeed?), Investigators (PI track record, team expertise, are there skill gaps — note the PI has strong hepatology background but limited in vivo metabolic phenotyping experience), Innovation (what is conceptually new versus incremental?), Approach (Aim 1-3 evaluation — identify the riskiest aim and whether the contingency plans are adequate), and Environment (institutional resources). Then write two Weaknesses that are substantive enough to prevent funding without revision and two Strengths. Use NIH-style language ('The application addresses a critical knowledge gap', 'The approach is well-reasoned', 'A notable weakness is the absence of...'). Approximately 700 words.","tags":["peer-review","NIH-study-section","grant-review","NAFLD","FXR"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:45:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-59","title":"Retraction Decision: Evaluating Evidence of Data Fabrication","prompt":"You are a research integrity officer at a university receiving a formal allegation that three figures in a published Journal of Neuroscience paper from your institution may contain fabricated electrophysiology traces. The complainant has provided: (1) side-by-side comparisons showing that four patch-clamp traces labelled as independent cells are pixel-identical except for minor brightness adjustments; (2) lab notebook records from another researcher suggesting the experiments were conducted on dates when the reported equipment was under maintenance; (3) an email thread in which the first author asked a colleague to 'clean up the traces a bit'. Write a formal Investigation Plan document covering: the composition of the investigation committee (internal members plus two external experts with no competing interests), the evidence collection process (securing all original raw files from the microscope acquisition computer, preservation of electronic lab notebooks, interview protocol for all authors), the standards of evidence required (the 'preponderance of evidence' vs 'clear and convincing evidence' standard), the timeline (30-day preliminary inquiry followed by formal investigation within 120 days per ORI guidelines), how to handle the ongoing citation and use of the paper during investigation (consider requesting the journal issue an Expression of Concern), and the possible outcomes (retraction with or without statement of cause, correction, or exoneration). Reference ORI's Procedures for Cases of Research Misconduct 2022.","tags":["peer-review","research-integrity","retraction","data-fabrication","ORI"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:50:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-60","title":"Critique of a Preprint: Rapid Assessment Framework","prompt":"You are a senior virologist contributing to an ASAPbio rapid preprint review of a bioRxiv preprint claiming that a newly identified coronavirus surface protein variant in horseshoe bats in Yunnan province shows ACE2 receptor binding affinity comparable to SARS-CoV-2, based on cryo-EM structural data and pseudovirus entry assays in HEK293T cells. Write a structured rapid review (500 words) covering: (1) the central claim and whether the presented data support it — specifically evaluate whether pseudovirus assays in HEK293T cells overexpressing human ACE2 are sufficient to predict zoonotic spillover risk or whether primary airway organoid data are required; (2) structural analysis quality — were the cryo-EM maps deposited in the Electron Microscopy Data Bank (EMDB) and were the resolution and refinement statistics reported according to PDB validation standards; (3) phylogenetic analysis — is the ML phylogenetic tree appropriately bootstrapped (1000 replicates), is the outgroup appropriate, and are recombination events tested using RDP5 software; (4) public health communication — does the abstract accurately represent the limitations of the pseudovirus system without overstating pandemic risk, and does it include a responsible disclosure statement regarding pathogen sequence submission to GenBank. Rate the preprint as: Ready to read with caveats / Needs revision / Not suitable for public dissemination.","tags":["peer-review","preprint","ASAPbio","virology","coronavirus"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:55:00Z","featured":false},

  // --- 7. LAB MANAGEMENT & RESEARCH ETHICS (sci-61 to sci-70) ---
  {"id":"sci-61","title":"IRB Full Protocol: Randomised Trial of a Behavioural Intervention","prompt":"You are a research administrator helping a clinical psychologist write a full IRB application for a two-arm RCT comparing a smartphone-delivered cognitive behavioural therapy (CBT) app versus waitlist control for reducing generalised anxiety disorder (GAD) symptoms over 8 weeks in university students (target n=160, 18-25 years). Write the following IRB application sections in full: (1) Research Background and Scientific Justification (200 words): cite the 31% prevalence of GAD in US college students (ACHA 2022 data), the evidence base for digital CBT (ICBT), and the gap addressed; (2) Participant Selection Criteria: inclusion (PHQ-GAD-7 ≥10, owns iOS/Android smartphone, English-speaking) and exclusion (current psychotherapy, active suicidal ideation assessed by C-SSRS item 4-5, severe MDD comorbidity by PHQ-9 ≥20); (3) Recruitment and Consent Process: describe online screening, two-stage consent (online informed consent then in-person confirmation), and the cooling-off period; (4) Risk Assessment: enumerate risks (distress from symptom monitoring, app data breach, inadvertent unblinding) and mitigations; (5) Data Safety Monitoring Plan: stopping rule if C-SSRS active ideation with intent is disclosed, mandatory referral pathway to university counselling services within 24 hours; (6) Statistical Analysis Plan: ANCOVA of GAD-7 at 8 weeks adjusted for baseline, intention-to-treat population.","tags":["lab-management","IRB","RCT","digital-health","mental-health"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-62","title":"Postdoc Onboarding Plan and Lab Expectations Document","prompt":"You are a PI at an R1 university medical school establishing a written Lab Expectations and Onboarding document for a new postdoctoral researcher starting in your epigenomics lab. Write the complete document (700 words) that covers: (1) Lab philosophy and culture — commitment to reproducible, open science; zero tolerance for data manipulation; no HARKing (Hypothesising After Results are Known); psychological safety to report mistakes without fear; (2) Working hours and availability — core hours policy (10am-4pm in-person expected, flexibility otherwise), PI office hours schedule, Slack communication norms (24-hour response expectation, no expectation of weekend replies); (3) Research expectations in months 1-3 — literature review deliverable, lab technique competency checklist, first data analysis script pushed to lab GitHub by week 8; (4) Data management — all raw data stored in lab server within 48h of acquisition, electronic lab notebook in Benchling with weekly entries, no data deletion without PI approval; (5) Authorship norms — reference to ICMJE criteria, explicit statement that postdoc will be first author on all papers arising from their primary project; (6) Career development — annual Individual Development Plan (IDP) using myIDP.sciencecareers.org, commitment to fund conference attendance once per year; (7) What to do when things go wrong — reporting line for concerns, access to ombudsperson.","tags":["lab-management","onboarding","postdoc","lab-culture","mentorship"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:05:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-63","title":"Biosafety Level 2 Lab Risk Assessment and SOP","prompt":"You are a university biosafety officer writing a BSL-2 Risk Assessment and accompanying Standard Operating Procedure for work with a recombinant lentiviral vector system (third-generation packaging system, VSV-G pseudotyped, replication-incompetent, expressing a GFP reporter) for transduction of primary human T cells. The assessment must comply with ABSA International Biosafety Guidelines and your institution's IBC registration requirements. Include: hazard identification (RG2 classification, route of exposure — needlestick or mucosal splash, biodistribution of VSV-G-pseudotyped vectors in accidental human exposure), risk matrix (3×3 likelihood × severity), mandatory engineering controls (Class II Type A2 biological safety cabinet, sealed-rotor centrifuge), PPE requirements (nitrile gloves double-gloving, lab coat, eye protection for ultracentrifugation steps), waste decontamination (10% bleach 30-minute contact time, autoclaving at 132°C for 30 minutes), emergency response procedure for needlestick (immediate first aid, incident reporting to biosafety office within 2 hours, occupational health HIV PEP assessment within 72 hours), and prohibited activities (mouth pipetting, eating, open-toed shoes, working alone with concentrated virus). Format as an institutional SOP with document control header.","tags":["lab-management","biosafety","BSL-2","lentiviral-vector","SOP"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:10:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-64","title":"Reproducibility Checklist for a Computational Paper","prompt":"You are a research software engineer and reproducibility advocate reviewing a computational biology paper before submission to PLOS Computational Biology. The paper presents a new algorithm for predicting protein-protein interaction interfaces from AlphaFold2 structural predictions, benchmarked on the DOCKGROUND database. Create a comprehensive reproducibility checklist with 25 items across five categories: (1) Code availability (5 items: GitHub repository with archival DOI via Zenodo, OSI-approved open-source licence, README with installation instructions, requirements.txt or conda environment.yml with pinned versions, continuous integration tests passing); (2) Data availability (5 items: all training/test data on Zenodo or Figshare, GenBank/PDB accession numbers for all sequences and structures, clear split of training/validation/test sets with no data leakage, cross-validation folds fixed with a random seed); (3) Methods reproducibility (5 items: hyperparameters reported in full, random seeds set and reported, all data preprocessing steps scripted with no manual steps, computational environment specified including GPU type and CUDA version); (4) Results reproducibility (5 items: all figures generated by scripts in the repository, tables generated programmatically, benchmark comparison scripts included, statistical tests scripted); (5) Documentation (5 items: tutorial notebook, API documentation, changelog, citation instructions, contact for questions). Flag each item as Pass/Fail for this paper based on the information typically missing in computational biology papers.","tags":["lab-management","reproducibility","computational-biology","open-science","PLOS"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:15:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-65","title":"Animal Ethics Application: 3Rs Framework","prompt":"You are a veterinary scientist and animal ethics committee member helping a PI write an Animal Ethics Committee (AEC) application for a study using 96 BALB/c mice to test the efficacy of a novel nanoparticle-formulated chemotherapy agent in a syngeneic 4T1 mammary tumour model. The application must be written under the 3Rs framework (Replace, Reduce, Refine) as required by the Animals (Scientific Procedures) Act 1986 (UK) and the ETS 123 Appendix A guidelines. Write the 3Rs section in full: Replacement — explain why in vitro 4T1 cell culture experiments (conducted in Aims 1-2) and ex vivo tumour organoids are insufficient to address the primary efficacy endpoint and why in vivo tumour growth kinetics are required; Reduction — justify n=8 per group across 12 groups using a power calculation (effect size d=0.8, α=0.05, power=0.80 → n=26 per comparison, reduced to n=8 per group with internal positive control normalisation and reduced group design) and explain the use of tumour growth inhibition as a continuous variable rather than binary endpoint; Refinement — detail humane endpoints (tumour size 15mm diameter OR 15% body weight loss OR early endpoint criteria), analgesic protocol (buprenorphine 0.1 mg/kg s.c. q12h post-implantation), housing enrichment plan (nesting material, environmental enrichment, social housing in groups of 4). Approximately 550 words.","tags":["lab-management","animal-ethics","3Rs","tumour-model","ASPA"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:20:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-66","title":"Research Misconduct: Navigating a Complaint as a Trainee","prompt":"You are a research integrity officer providing guidance to a third-year PhD student who has witnessed their supervisor selectively exclude three data points from a key figure in a manuscript under revision, and the supervisor has told the student 'those are outliers, we don't need them' without performing any formal outlier test or noting the exclusion in the Methods. The student is concerned this constitutes research misconduct but is afraid of retaliation. Write a structured advisory document (600 words) for the student that covers: (1) how to document the concern contemporaneously and securely (personal encrypted notebook, screenshot of version history in the manuscript tracking system, dated emails to self); (2) the difference between research misconduct (fabrication, falsification, plagiarism per ORI definition) versus questionable research practices — explain where the described behaviour falls and the significance of that categorisation; (3) the confidential reporting options available (institutional ombudsperson, departmental graduate director, institutional research integrity officer, national body such as UKRIO in the UK or ORI in the US); (4) legal protections against retaliation under the Public Interest Disclosure Act 1998 (UK) and National Science Foundation policies; (5) the likely investigation timeline and outcomes; (6) psychological support resources. Empathetic, practical, non-alarmist tone.","tags":["lab-management","research-misconduct","PhD-student","research-integrity","whistleblowing"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:25:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-67","title":"Lab Budget Planning: Managing a £500k Research Grant","prompt":"You are a PI and research finance officer preparing a detailed 36-month budget management plan for a Wellcome Trust Investigator Award of £500,000 (direct costs). The project involves two postdoctoral researchers, one PhD student, multi-omics experiments (ChIP-seq, RNA-seq, mass spectrometry proteomics), and a planned collaboration with a biotech company for compound screening. Draft the detailed budget breakdown by category: Personnel (Postdoc 1 at Grade 7 spine 31, Postdoc 2 at Grade 7 spine 29, PhD student stipend at UKRI rate £18,622/year, employer NICs at 13.8%, 4% employer pension; total personnel 3-year cost), Consumables (Year 1: cell culture and molecular biology consumables £28,000, sequencing library preparation £15,000, mass spectrometry sample preparation £8,000; contingency for reagent price increases), Collaboration and Subcontract (£30,000 to the biotech partner for compound screening as a service agreement), Travel and Dissemination (two international conferences per postdoc per year at £1,800 each, open access publication fees reserve of £12,000 over 3 years), Equipment (no major capital, minor equipment budget of £5,000/year), and a 10% contingency reserve. Explain the quarterly forecasting process and how to handle underspend at year-end under Wellcome's grant conditions.","tags":["lab-management","grant-budget","Wellcome-Trust","research-finance","postdoc"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:30:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-68","title":"Conflict of Interest Policy: Writing a Disclosure Statement","prompt":"You are a research ethics expert helping a senior cardiologist write a comprehensive conflict of interest (COI) disclosure statement for a clinical practice guideline she is co-authoring for the European Society of Cardiology (ESC) on the management of atrial fibrillation. Over the past three years she has: received a €45,000 unrestricted research grant from Bayer AG (manufacturer of rivaroxaban), given 6 paid lectures on AF management for Pfizer (developer of apixaban) totalling €12,000 in honoraria, holds a consultancy agreement with BMS worth €8,000/year for advisory board participation, and owns 500 shares in AstraZeneca inherited from her father (current value €22,000). Write: (1) the full financial COI disclosure paragraph in the format required by the ESC Guidelines COI Form 2023; (2) an assessment of which COIs are direct (relating to drugs covered in the guideline) versus indirect; (3) a recommendation on which guideline sections she should be recused from voting on under ESC policy (direct COI recusal rule); (4) a brief public-facing disclosure statement for the published guideline; (5) advice on whether the share ownership requires divestment before participation under ESC rules. Reference ICMJE COI disclosure requirements.","tags":["lab-management","conflict-of-interest","ESC","clinical-guidelines","disclosure"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:35:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-69","title":"Lab Meeting Structure: Mentoring Through Data Presentation","prompt":"You are an experienced PI designing a weekly lab meeting structure for a 12-person research group (3 postdocs, 5 PhD students, 2 master's students, 1 research technician, 1 visiting student) working on stem cell biology and regenerative medicine. Write a complete Lab Meeting Guide document (600 words) that specifies: the 90-minute agenda template (15 min: announcements and shared resource updates; 45 min: primary data presentation by one group member; 15 min: journal club of one recent paper selected by the presenter; 15 min: problem-solving whiteboard session on a stalled experiment nominated by any group member); expectations for the data presenter (minimum one new dataset per presentation, all figures shown in final publication-quality format, all n numbers visible, no 'I don't know' responses — prepare answers to the three most obvious questions in advance, present controls before treatment data); journal club expectations (presenter must identify one methodological flaw in the paper and one experiment they would add); a policy on 'safe space' presentations (designated rotation for presenting failed experiments without blame); evaluation rubric for PhD student presentations used for annual progress reviews; and a buddy system pairing each PhD student with a postdoc for pre-lab meeting rehearsal 48 hours in advance.","tags":["lab-management","lab-meeting","mentoring","PhD-training","stem-cells"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:40:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-70","title":"Data Sharing Agreement for Multi-Institutional Study","prompt":"You are a research data governance specialist drafting the Data Sharing Agreement (DSA) between three UK university hospitals participating in a multi-centre retrospective cohort study of hospital-acquired Clostridioides difficile infection (CDI) using linked electronic health records (EHR data from Epic and TPP SystmOne), microbiome sequencing data, and antibiotic prescribing records for 12,500 patients over 5 years. Write the key contractual sections of the DSA following the NHS Information Governance Alliance model: (1) Data Controller and Data Processor designations (lead site as Controller, receiving sites as Joint Controllers for their own data, central bioinformatics facility as Processor); (2) Data minimisation schedule listing the 28 specific data fields shared, confirming no patient names, NHS numbers, or postcodes below LSOA level are included; (3) Legal basis for processing under UK GDPR Article 6(1)(e) and Article 9(2)(j) for special category health data, with reference to Section 251 NHS Act 2006 approval; (4) Data security requirements (minimum NHS DSP Toolkit compliance, encrypted transfer via SFTP with SHA-256 hashing, no data on personal devices); (5) Data breach notification procedure (72-hour notification to ICO and all parties); (6) Data retention and destruction schedule (5 years post-publication, then secure deletion with certificate). Approximately 600 words.","tags":["lab-management","data-sharing","GDPR","NHS","multi-centre"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:45:00Z","featured":false},

  // --- 8. SCIENCE COMMUNICATION (sci-71 to sci-80) ---
  {"id":"sci-71","title":"Press Release: Translating Breakthrough Research for Journalists","prompt":"You are a science communications officer at a Russell Group university writing a press release for a paper published in Nature Medicine reporting that a liquid biopsy test measuring 12 circulating tumour DNA (ctDNA) methylation markers achieves 89% sensitivity and 94% specificity for detecting pancreatic cancer at Stage I-II in a prospective study of 2,300 high-risk individuals. Write the complete press release following the Association of British Science Writers (ABSW) standard format: headline (plain language, present tense, no jargon — maximum 12 words), standfirst (2-sentence summary expanding the headline), body (5 paragraphs: 1. the finding in plain language; 2. why pancreatic cancer is particularly difficult to detect and why Stage I-II detection matters for survival; 3. how the test works in non-technical language — a blood draw that looks for chemical signatures on tumour DNA; 4. quote from lead researcher and quote from an independent expert; 5. next steps — Phase III validation trial beginning Q1 2025 at 8 UK NHS Trusts), boilerplate paragraphs about the university and the funder (Cancer Research UK), and an 'Editor's Notes' section with the full paper citation and media contact details. Avoid hyperbole — do not use the word 'breakthrough', 'game-changer', or 'cure'.","tags":["science-communication","press-release","Nature-Medicine","cancer","liquid-biopsy"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:50:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-72","title":"Public Lecture Script: Explaining CRISPR to a Non-Expert Audience","prompt":"You are a molecular biologist preparing a 20-minute public lecture on CRISPR-Cas9 gene editing for a Cheltenham Science Festival audience of 500 adults with no scientific background. Write the complete lecture script (1,500 words) structured as follows: opening hook (a brief story about a child with sickle cell disease who received the first approved CRISPR therapy, Casgevy, in 2023 — 2 minutes), the 'copy-paste analogy' explanation of how CRISPR works without using the words 'nuclease', 'endonuclease', or 'genome' in the first 5 minutes, transition from analogy to mechanism (guide RNA as the address label, Cas9 as the molecular scissors — 4 minutes), three real applications explained in plain English (inherited blood disorders, agricultural disease resistance in wheat, potential HIV cure — 6 minutes), honest discussion of risks and ethics in accessible language: germline editing, off-target cuts, equitable access — 4 minutes, and a closing Q&A preparation sentence inviting questions. The script must not condescend — assume your audience is intelligent but non-specialist. Include three suggested pause points for audience questions, and two suggested demonstration props (a physical analogy using scissors and a paper strand, a short video clip of Cas9 cutting DNA in molecular simulation).","tags":["science-communication","public-lecture","CRISPR","public-engagement","Cheltenham"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:55:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-73","title":"Policy Brief: Translating Research Evidence for Government","prompt":"You are a science policy advisor at the Parliamentary Office of Science and Technology (POST) writing a POSTnote briefing for members of parliament on the current evidence base for restricting ultra-violet tanning bed use in under-18s in England. The briefing will inform a parliamentary debate on extending the England-only ban proposed in the Health and Care Act 2022. Write the complete POSTnote (800 words, accessible English, no jargon) covering: background (tanning bed prevalence among UK adolescents, UV radiation as a Group 1 IARC carcinogen, link to cutaneous melanoma), summary of the evidence (systematic review data on relative risk of melanoma with adolescent UV exposure: RR 1.87, 95% CI 1.50-2.32; data on tanning bed use among 14-17 year olds in England: 7.4% annual prevalence per 2021 CLAHRC survey), international comparisons (Brazil, Australia, France, Germany all with full bans, Scotland's Children and Young People Act 2019), current legislative gap in England (voluntary code of practice versus statutory ban), industry and civil liberties perspectives (Freedom to Tan lobby, employment impact on salons), and a balanced options section presenting three policy scenarios: status quo, statutory ban under-18s only, full ban with sunset clause. Flag evidence quality using POSTnote's established traffic light system.","tags":["science-communication","policy-brief","POST","tanning-beds","evidence-based-policy"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-74","title":"Conference Talk Abstract: Submitting to ISMB","prompt":"You are a computational biologist submitting a talk abstract to ISMB/ECCB 2025 (Intelligent Systems for Molecular Biology) for a presentation describing a new deep learning method, ProteinContactNet, that predicts residue-residue contact maps in multi-domain proteins more accurately than AlphaFold2 in the disordered region benchmark. Write the 250-word abstract for the Protein Structure and Function track following ISMB's submission guidelines: title (concise, specific, no abbreviations in title), author list placeholder, one-sentence motivation (why existing methods fail on multi-domain disordered proteins), one-paragraph Methods summary (transformer-based architecture trained on 45,000 PDB structures with multi-scale attention heads, trained on A100 GPUs using PyTorch 2.1 with mixed precision), one-paragraph Results (precision at L/5 contacts: ProteinContactNet 0.76 vs AlphaFold2 0.59 on the DisProt-2024 test set; superior performance maintained across four SCOP fold classes), one-sentence statement of availability (code and weights available at github.com/yourlab/ProteinContactNet under MIT licence, model deposited on HuggingFace Model Hub), and a concluding sentence on the biological impact (enabling structure prediction for 23% of the human proteome that remains in the intrinsically disordered category). Word count exactly 250.","tags":["science-communication","conference-abstract","ISMB","deep-learning","protein-structure"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:05:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-75","title":"Science Blog Post: Explaining a Retraction to the Public","prompt":"You are a science journalist writing a 700-word blog post for The Conversation explaining a high-profile retraction of a 2022 Nature paper that claimed to have synthesised a room-temperature superconductor (LK-99). The post must: explain what superconductivity is in one paragraph using only everyday analogies (no Meissner effect, no Cooper pairs in the first paragraph), explain what was originally claimed and why it generated global excitement, explain what went wrong in the peer review process (post-publication replication failure by 17 independent groups, subsequent identification of data selectivity in specific resistance measurements), use this case to explain how science self-corrects without undermining public trust in science generally, and address the specific concern readers may have: 'if peer review missed this, how can we trust scientific papers?'. The post must be fair to the original authors (they may have made honest errors), explain the difference between misconduct and failed replication, and end with a constructive message about preprint culture and the replication ecosystem. Target audience: educated non-scientist. Do not use the word 'flawed', 'debunked', or 'fake'.","tags":["science-communication","science-blogging","The-Conversation","retraction","superconductor"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:10:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-76","title":"Social Media Science Communication: Thread and Video Script","prompt":"You are a science communicator designing a multi-platform social media campaign to explain the findings of a new NEJM paper showing that a once-yearly subcutaneous injection of an siRNA therapy (inclisiran) reduces LDL cholesterol by 52% and reduces major adverse cardiovascular events by 26% over 3 years in 9,800 patients with established atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease. Design: (1) an 8-post Twitter/X thread (each post ≤280 characters, no jargon, with a logical narrative arc from 'why cholesterol matters' to 'what this means for patients' — specify which posts should include a graph, a diagram, or a patient story); (2) a 60-second Instagram Reel script with visual cues for each 10-second segment (avoid showing any patient imagery not available under Creative Commons, use animation descriptions instead); (3) a LinkedIn post targeting cardiologists and GPs (300 words, professional register, link to the paper, comment on implementation barriers in NHS formulary approval timelines); (4) a TikTok hook (first 3 seconds must stop the scroll — propose two alternative hooks and explain which is more effective for TikTok's algorithm); (5) a suggested hashtag strategy for each platform. Avoid health misinformation risks — do not imply the therapy replaces statins.","tags":["science-communication","social-media","cardiovascular","NEJM","siRNA"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:15:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-77","title":"Science Exhibit Design: Interactive Museum Display","prompt":"You are a science education designer commissioned by the Wellcome Collection to design an interactive museum exhibit on antibiotic resistance for a mixed-age audience (target: families with children aged 8+ and adult independent visitors). Write the complete exhibit design brief (700 words) covering: (1) Educational objectives — three specific learning outcomes (visitors will be able to explain horizontal gene transfer using a physical card-swap game; visitors will understand that antibiotic resistance is a natural evolutionary process accelerated by overuse; visitors will leave knowing one personal action they can take); (2) Four interactive stations with detailed descriptions: Station A (Giant Petri Dish floor projection where visitors 'become bacteria' and must dodge antibiotic zones, projected using Reactable technology), Station B (Antibiotic Timeline — physical timeline from Fleming 1928 to the last new antibiotic class approved 1987 to 2024 with no new classes), Station C (Real clinical case study touchscreen: a fictional 7-year-old with a resistant UTI — visitors make treatment decisions and see real-world consequences), Station D (Action Wall: take a pledge card, see collective pledges from previous visitors); (3) Accessibility requirements (BSL video interpretation, large print, tactile elements for visual impairment, adjustable heights); (4) Evaluation plan (entry/exit surveys with three knowledge questions, dwell time tracking by anonymous RFID).","tags":["science-communication","museum-exhibit","antibiotic-resistance","science-education","Wellcome"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:20:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-78","title":"Researcher Media Training: Handling Hostile Interview Questions","prompt":"You are a media trainer preparing a climate scientist for a live BBC Radio 4 interview about a new Nature Climate Change paper showing that global mean surface temperature has already reached 1.5°C above pre-industrial baseline in annual mean for the first time in 2024. The interviewer is known for adversarial questioning and representing climate sceptic perspectives. Write a media training document (600 words) covering: (1) three likely hostile questions and model answers for each (Q: 'Isn't the 1.5°C target already missed, so there's no point cutting emissions?'; Q: 'Your paper uses adjusted temperature records — how do we know they aren't manipulated?'; Q: 'You've been wrong before about tipping points — why should we trust this now?'); (2) the 'bridging technique' — how to acknowledge a question, give a short factual response, and pivot back to the key message without appearing evasive; (3) the top three messages to land regardless of the questions asked (write them in 20 words or fewer each); (4) what to do if the interviewer misstates a fact — how to correct firmly but not combatively; (5) how to handle 'false balance' framing where the interviewer implies the scientific community is divided when 97% consensus exists. Include specific example phrases to use and phrases to avoid.","tags":["science-communication","media-training","climate","BBC","press-interview"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:25:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-79","title":"Infographic Design: Explaining a Clinical Trial to Patients","prompt":"You are a health literacy expert and graphic designer creating a patient-facing infographic to explain a Phase III clinical trial of a new oral treatment for relapsing-remitting multiple sclerosis (RRMS) that potential participants will be recruited into via UK MS Society patient panels. The infographic will be on a single A4 page and must meet Plain English Campaign Crystal Mark standards (Flesch-Kincaid reading level ≤8, no medical jargon without immediate plain-language definition). Design the complete content and layout: (1) Header section: 'Could this trial help you?' with inclusion criteria in bullet points using plain language (diagnosed with relapsing MS, had at least one relapse in the past 2 years, aged 18-55); (2) What will happen to you? — illustrated timeline showing: Screening visit → 2-week run-in → Randomisation (50:50 chance of active pill or placebo) → 24 months of monthly clinic visits → Final assessment; (3) What are the risks? — two most common side effects from Phase II data (nausea 18%, mild headache 12%) with percentage visualised as icon arrays (10×10 person icons); (4) What are the benefits? — Phase II annualised relapse rate reduction 47% over placebo, visualised as before/after icon array; (5) Your rights box: you can leave at any time; your care will not be affected; plain-language contact details. Specify font (Arial minimum 12pt), colour scheme (high contrast for accessibility), and alt-text for each image.","tags":["science-communication","patient-information","clinical-trial","health-literacy","MS"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:30:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-80","title":"Science Festival Panel Discussion: Facilitation Guide","prompt":"You are a science festival director preparing the facilitation guide for a 90-minute panel discussion at the Edinburgh International Science Festival titled 'Should we edit human embryos? CRISPR, ethics, and the future of the human genome'. The panel includes a developmental geneticist, a bioethicist, a disability rights advocate, and a policy regulator from the HFEA. Write the complete facilitator's guide (700 words) covering: (1) pre-panel preparation — briefing panellists on the discussion norms (no jargon without explaining it, time allocation per panellist, the facilitator will interrupt if time runs over); (2) opening provocation question to frame the debate (write two alternative opening questions and recommend one, explaining why it opens discussion rather than closing it); (3) five prepared discussion questions sequenced from accessible (What is CRISPR and what are the recent milestones?) to nuanced (Who should have the right to make decisions about germline editing for future generations who cannot consent?) to confrontational (Is the disability community's opposition to 'correction' editing a legitimate constraint on scientific progress?); (4) how to handle audience hostility or emotional responses (specific de-escalation language); (5) three 'bridging questions' to redirect the discussion if it becomes too technical or too abstract; (6) closing synthesis question that ensures every panellist's perspective is represented. Include stage direction notes.","tags":["science-communication","panel-discussion","CRISPR","ethics","Edinburgh-Science-Festival"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:35:00Z","featured":false},

  // --- 9. COMPUTATIONAL & BIOINFORMATICS (sci-81 to sci-90) ---
  {"id":"sci-81","title":"Genomics Pipeline QC: Identifying and Resolving Failures","prompt":"You are a senior bioinformatician at a genomics core facility troubleshooting a whole-exome sequencing (WES) pipeline for 48 patient samples sequenced on the Illumina NovaSeq X Plus. The pipeline runs BWA-MEM2 alignment to GRCh38, GATK4 HaplotypeCaller for variant calling, and VEP for annotation. The MultiQC report reveals three concerning QC failures: (1) samples 12, 23, and 41 have on-target coverage below 80× (minimum required for clinical variant calling), with mean depths of 62×, 71×, and 58×; (2) samples 08 and 29 have adapter contamination above 15% by FastQC, suggesting library preparation failed; (3) samples 33-36 (a sequential batch) show a GC content bimodal distribution at 40% and 65% indicating possible DNA degradation from the FFPE source material. For each QC failure, describe: the specific metric thresholds used, the root cause analysis process (was the failure wet-lab or bioinformatic?), the decision tree for remediation (re-sequence vs proceed with caveats vs exclude), and how to document the QC decision for a CLIA-compliant clinical genomics report. Provide the specific MultiQC/FastQC module and threshold for each failure. Approximately 550 words.","tags":["bioinformatics","whole-exome-sequencing","GATK","QC","genomics-pipeline"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:40:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-82","title":"Python for Scientific Data Analysis: Pandas and SciPy Workflow","prompt":"You are a scientific Python instructor teaching a workshop on data analysis for life scientists. Write a complete, annotated Python tutorial (with code and explanations) for analysing a microplate reader dataset: a 96-well plate absorbance assay measuring NAD+/NADH ratio by a fluorescence-based cycling assay in 24 treatment conditions (8 wells per condition, including 8 blank wells and 8 standard curve wells). The tutorial must cover: (1) loading the raw CSV export from a BMG CLARIOstar plate reader using pandas with handling of the irregular header format; (2) plate map construction as a dictionary and mapping well IDs to treatment conditions; (3) blank subtraction and background correction; (4) standard curve fitting using scipy.optimize.curve_fit with a 4PL model (include the 4PL function definition); (5) interpolation of sample concentrations from the standard curve with propagation of uncertainty; (6) one-way ANOVA with scipy.stats.f_oneway followed by Tukey HSD post-hoc test using statsmodels; (7) visualisation as a violin plot with individual data points using seaborn with all formatting required for a Nature Methods figure (8pt Helvetica font, no top/right spines, significance bars added with statannotations). Provide complete runnable code with comments. Specify package versions used (pandas 2.1, scipy 1.11, seaborn 0.13).","tags":["bioinformatics","Python","pandas","scipy","data-analysis"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:45:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-83","title":"Genome Assembly Quality Assessment","prompt":"You are a genomics bioinformatician evaluating the quality of a de novo genome assembly for a novel deep-sea extremophile bacterium sequenced using PacBio HiFi long reads (20× coverage, mean read length 15 kb) and Illumina short reads (100× coverage, 150 bp paired-end) with hybrid assembly using Hifiasm and polished with Pilon. Write the complete quality assessment protocol: (1) Assembly statistics using QUAST — explain which metrics matter most (N50, L50, number of contigs, total assembly size compared to estimated genome size by flow cytometry, largest contig) and give exemplar values for a high-quality bacterial assembly (N50 > 500 kb, <50 contigs); (2) Completeness assessment using BUSCO v5 with the bacteria_odb10 lineage — explain the C/S/D/F/M breakdown and what a 98.2% complete single-copy BUSCO score means; (3) Base-level accuracy assessment using Merqury with the Illumina reads as a k-mer database — interpret the QV (quality value) score (QV > 40 indicates fewer than 1 error per 10,000 bases); (4) Contamination screening using NCBI FCS-GX to remove contigs matching human, viral, or common environmental contaminants; (5) genome annotation using Prokka v1.14 and KEGG Automatic Annotation Server (KAAS) to characterise novel biosynthetic gene clusters using antiSMASH 7. Generate a report template for publication in a Genome Announcement.","tags":["bioinformatics","genome-assembly","BUSCO","PacBio","QUAST"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:50:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-84","title":"GWAS Analysis Pipeline: From QC to Functional Annotation","prompt":"You are a statistical geneticist leading a genome-wide association study (GWAS) of systolic blood pressure in 24,500 individuals from the UK Biobank (European ancestry) genotyped on the Affymetrix Axiom UK Biobank array with imputation to the HRC reference panel. Write the complete analytical pipeline: (1) Genotype QC in PLINK2 — sample-level filters (call rate >98%, sex discordance by F-statistic, heterozygosity outliers ±3 SD from mean, related individuals removed by KING kinship coefficient >0.0625 for first-degree relatives), SNP-level filters (MAF >1%, HWE p > 1×10⁻⁶ in controls, call rate >98%); (2) population stratification — principal component analysis with 1000 Genomes reference using flashPCA, plot first 10 PCs, include PCs 1-10 as covariates in all association models; (3) Association testing using BOLT-LMM for mixed-model association accounting for cryptic relatedness, with covariates age, sex, BMI, smoking status, antihypertensive medication use; (4) results interpretation — Manhattan plot and QQ-plot, genomic inflation factor λ, genome-wide significance threshold (p < 5×10⁻⁸), regional association plots using LocusZoom for the top 5 loci; (5) post-GWAS functional annotation — MAGMA gene-level analysis, FUMA platform for SNP annotation against GTEx v8 eQTL data, LD score regression for heritability and genetic correlation with related traits. Name all software with version numbers.","tags":["bioinformatics","GWAS","UK-Biobank","statistical-genetics","PLINK"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:55:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-85","title":"Protein Structure Prediction and Validation with AlphaFold2","prompt":"You are a structural bioinformatician helping a biochemistry team predict, validate, and interpret the structure of a novel 380 amino acid bacterial effector protein (Uniprot accession placeholder) with no homologues in the PDB above 20% sequence identity. Write the complete computational workflow: (1) AlphaFold2 prediction using ColabFold v1.5.5 with full database search (UniRef90 + BFD + MGnify MSA), template search disabled to test ab initio accuracy, all 5 model variants run, specify how to interpret the per-residue pLDDT score (>90 = high confidence, 70-90 = moderate, <70 = low confidence/disordered) and the inter-residue Predicted Aligned Error (PAE) matrix for domain boundary identification; (2) model validation against experimental data (cryo-EM density map at 3.8 Å resolution) using PHENIX real-space refinement and MolProbity for Ramachandran statistics (target: >95% favoured, <0.5% outliers); (3) structural comparison using DALI server to search the PDB for structural homologues even in the absence of sequence homology; (4) active site prediction using FPocket and DoGSiteScorer to identify druggable pockets; (5) molecular dynamics simulation setup in GROMACS 2023 using the CHARMM36m force field to assess the conformational flexibility of a disordered loop in the active site over a 100 ns simulation with root-mean-square deviation and fluctuation analysis. Specify all software and parameter files.","tags":["bioinformatics","AlphaFold2","protein-structure","molecular-dynamics","GROMACS"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-86","title":"Metagenomics Pipeline: Shotgun Sequencing Analysis","prompt":"You are a metagenomics specialist analysing shotgun sequencing data from 40 stool samples (20 from patients with active Crohn's disease, 20 from age-matched healthy controls) sequenced at 10M reads per sample (2×150 bp, NovaSeq 6000). Write the complete analytical workflow with software names and versions: (1) host read removal using Bowtie2 v2.5 against GRCh38, reporting depletion efficiency; (2) quality control with Trim Galore (Phred Q > 20, minimum 75 bp post-trimming); (3) taxonomic profiling using MetaPhlAn4 with the mpa_vJan21_CHOCOPhlAnSGB_202103 database — explain the relative abundance output and how to convert to CLR-transformed values for statistical analysis; (4) functional profiling using HUMAnN3 with the UniRef90 protein database — explain the pathway coverage and abundance outputs and how to interpret unmapped reads fraction above 40%; (5) alpha diversity analysis using Shannon entropy and Chao1 richness with phyloseq in R, Wilcoxon test between groups with FDR correction; (6) beta diversity using Bray-Curtis dissimilarity matrix and PERMANOVA in vegan (adonis2) to test group differences; (7) differential taxa testing using MaAsLin2 with linear mixed-effects model adjusting for BMI, antibiotics in past 3 months, and sequencing batch; (8) co-abundance network construction using SPIEC-EASI with SPRING normalization. Produce code for steps 5-7.","tags":["bioinformatics","metagenomics","gut-microbiome","Crohn's","MetaPhlAn"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:05:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-87","title":"R Shiny App for Interactive Research Data Visualisation","prompt":"You are a data science educator teaching biomedical researchers how to build interactive dashboards. Write a complete tutorial for building an R Shiny application that allows researchers to interactively explore a clinical proteomics dataset: 480 plasma samples from four disease groups (healthy, early AD, late AD, MCI) each with 92 protein biomarkers measured by Olink Proximity Extension Assay. The app must have four tabs: (1) Data Overview tab — interactive data table using DT package showing protein concentrations with search, sort, and export to CSV; (2) Volcano Plot tab — server-side ggplot2 volcano plot with a selectInput dropdown for which group comparison to display (Healthy vs MCI, Healthy vs early AD, Healthy vs late AD), a sliderInput for the log2FC and -log10(p) threshold axes, and reactive point labels for the top 5 proteins by -log10 adjusted p-value using ggrepel; (3) Heatmap tab — interactive heatmap using ComplexHeatmap rendered via renderPlot with k-means clustering and user-selectable number of clusters (2-6) via sliderInput; (4) Biomarker Correlation tab — select any two proteins by name using selectInput controls and display a scatter plot with Spearman correlation coefficient and 95% CI. Write the complete ui.R and server.R code with explanatory comments throughout. Specify R version 4.3.2 and all package versions.","tags":["bioinformatics","R-Shiny","proteomics","data-visualisation","interactive"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:10:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-88","title":"Phylogenetic Analysis: Building and Interpreting Trees","prompt":"You are a molecular phylogeneticist teaching a graduate course on evolutionary genomics. Write a step-by-step tutorial (700 words) for constructing and interpreting a maximum likelihood phylogenetic tree for 28 hemagglutinin (HA) protein sequences of influenza A H3N2 viruses collected between 1968 and 2024 from NCBI GenBank, aiming to track the antigenic evolution of the virus and inform vaccine strain selection. Cover: (1) sequence retrieval from NCBI using Entrez Direct command-line tools with BioProject PRJNA485481; (2) multiple sequence alignment using MAFFT v7 with the L-INS-i algorithm (recommended for sequences with conserved core and variable ends); (3) alignment trimming using trimAl v1.4 with the -automated1 option and inspection of the alignment length before and after trimming; (4) model selection using ModelTest-NG to identify the best-fit substitution model (typically GTR+G+I for HA sequences) — explain what the Akaike Information Criterion (AIC) score means in this context; (5) maximum likelihood tree construction using IQ-TREE2 with 1000 ultrafast bootstrap replicates — explain why bootstrap values >95 indicate strong support and why they differ from Bayesian posterior probabilities; (6) tree visualisation using FigTree — explain how to root the tree using the 1968 Hong Kong pandemic strain as outgroup, annotate clades by WHO nomenclature (3C.2a, 3C.3a), and colour branches by year to show temporal clustering. Identify one sign of antigenic drift in the visualised tree.","tags":["bioinformatics","phylogenetics","influenza","IQ-TREE","maximum-likelihood"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:15:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-89","title":"ChIP-Seq Analysis: From Raw Reads to Binding Profiles","prompt":"You are a genomics bioinformatician writing the computational methods section and analysis guide for a ChIP-seq study characterising H3K27ac enhancer activity in human iPSC-derived cardiomyocytes treated with doxorubicin (cardiotoxic chemotherapy) versus vehicle control, using Input DNA as the control. The experiment used 50 bp single-end sequencing on a NextSeq 2000. Write the complete pipeline: (1) QC with FastQC v0.12 and MultiQC — key metrics: median Phred score >30, duplication rate expected 30-50% for ChIP (not concerning), adapter content <5%; (2) alignment with Bowtie2 v2.5 to GRCh38 — explain the --very-sensitive-local mode for ChIP-seq; (3) post-alignment QC using deepTools 3.5: Pearson correlation between replicates (r > 0.9 acceptable), fingerprint plot to assess signal-to-noise (Input should be a diagonal line; ChIP should inflect above diagonal); (4) peak calling with MACS3 using the Input as control, --format BAM --gsize hs --nomodel --extsize 200 for histone marks, FDR 0.05 threshold; (5) differential binding analysis using DiffBind v3 with DESeq2 backend, TMM normalisation, identify gained and lost H3K27ac peaks in doxorubicin versus control; (6) motif enrichment in differential peaks using HOMER findMotifsGenome.pl against the JASPAR 2024 database; (7) visualisation of the top locus (e.g., NPPA cardiac stress gene) as a genome browser track in IGV with BigWig signal tracks normalised to 1× coverage (RPGC). Specify all parameters.","tags":["bioinformatics","ChIP-seq","epigenomics","H3K27ac","deepTools"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:20:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-90","title":"Clinical Variant Interpretation: ACMG Guidelines","prompt":"You are a clinical molecular geneticist at an NHS Genomic Medicine Service laboratory interpreting a variant detected by whole-genome sequencing in a 6-year-old child with unexplained intellectual disability and dysmorphic features. The variant is a de novo missense substitution, c.1427G>A (p.Arg476His), in the KDM5C gene (OMIM #300534, X-linked, associated with Claes-Jensen syndrome). Walk through the complete ACMG/AMP 2015 variant classification framework and ClinGen refinements: (1) Apply each evidence criterion with your reasoning — PS2 (de novo, confirmed parentage), PP3 (REVEL score 0.92, CADD 28.3, above pathogenicity thresholds), PM1 (variant in KDM5C catalytic JmjC domain), PM2 (absent from gnomAD v4 exomes and genomes in hemizygous males), BS3 (check for functional data — none available, so this is absent), and PP4 (phenotype highly specific for KDM5C-related intellectual disability including low-set ears, hypotonia, and XLID); (2) combine evidence using the Bayesian classification framework to reach a final classification (Likely Pathogenic: PS2 + PM1 + PM2 + PP3 + PP4 = Likely Pathogenic, insufficient for Pathogenic without functional data); (3) write the clinical variant report section for the medical genetics team in plain clinical language, including the HGMD and ClinVar submission plan; (4) recommend appropriate parental carrier testing and genetic counselling next steps for the family. Use standard HGVS nomenclature throughout.","tags":["bioinformatics","clinical-genomics","ACMG","variant-interpretation","rare-disease"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:25:00Z","featured":false},

  // --- 10. INTERDISCIPLINARY & APPLIED RESEARCH (sci-91 to sci-100) ---
  {"id":"sci-91","title":"Translational Research Roadmap: From Bench to Phase I Trial","prompt":"You are a translational research director at an academic medical centre helping a team of molecular oncologists map their discovery — a novel allosteric inhibitor of mutant IDH1 (R132H) that restores normal α-ketoglutarate metabolism in glioma cells — onto a structured translational pathway toward a Phase I first-in-human clinical trial. Write a detailed Translational Research Roadmap (700 words) covering the key stages: (1) Target validation package — what additional in vitro data are required before IND-enabling studies (selectivity panel against 300 kinases, CETSA target engagement in glioma cell lines, CRISPR rescue experiment to confirm on-target mechanism, proteomics for off-target proteins); (2) Lead optimisation milestones — DMPK requirements (plasma half-life >4h in mice, oral bioavailability >30%, brain penetrance Kp,uu,brain >0.3, CYP inhibition panel), medicinal chemistry strategy to improve CNS penetrance by reducing polar surface area; (3) IND-enabling preclinical package — GLP toxicology studies (28-day repeat dose rat and dog, genotoxicity Ames and in vitro micronucleus, safety pharmacology battery per ICH S7A), efficacy in orthotopic U87-IDH1R132H intracranial mouse model with survival endpoint; (4) Phase I trial design — dose escalation design (3+3 or BOIN), patient population (IDH1-mutant grade 2-3 glioma), primary endpoint (MTD, RP2D), PK/PD biomarker (plasma 2-HG by LC-MS/MS), required IND sections per 21 CFR Part 312. Specify regulatory pathway and estimated timeline (36-48 months from current stage to IND filing).","tags":["translational-research","drug-development","IDH1","glioma","Phase-I"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:30:00Z","featured":true},

  {"id":"sci-92","title":"Patent Application: Drafting Claims for a Biotech Invention","prompt":"You are a patent attorney with expertise in biotechnology and molecular diagnostics working with academic inventors at a university technology transfer office. The invention is a novel lateral flow immunoassay (LFA) for point-of-care detection of sepsis using a combination of three salivary biomarkers (IL-6, procalcitonin, and CRP) that collectively achieve AUC 0.94 for SIRS-positive sepsis within 15 minutes without sample pre-processing. Write the Claims section of a patent application for this invention with: (1) one Independent Claim (Claim 1) that is as broad as possible while distinguishing over prior art — claim the combination of the three specific biomarkers in a single lateral flow format without specifying concentrations or antibody clones; (2) six Dependent Claims (Claims 2-7) that progressively narrow the invention: Claim 2 specifies the nitrocellulose membrane substrate; Claim 3 adds the colloidal gold nanoparticle label; Claim 4 specifies the concentration thresholds (IL-6 >200 pg/mL OR PCT >0.5 ng/mL OR CRP >10 mg/L triggers a positive result); Claim 5 adds a smartphone-readable optical reader; Claim 6 specifies the salivary sample collection device; Claim 7 covers a kit comprising Claims 1-6 with instructions for use. Use the precise language of patent claim drafting ('comprising', 'wherein', 'configured to') and explain the commercial freedom-to-operate considerations relative to Abbott's i-STAT sepsis panel prior art.","tags":["translational-research","patent","lateral-flow","sepsis-diagnostics","technology-transfer"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:35:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-93","title":"Industry-Academia Collaboration: Due Diligence Report","prompt":"You are a senior scientist at a mid-size oncology biotech company conducting scientific due diligence on a potential licensing agreement for a university spin-out's antibody-drug conjugate (ADC) targeting HER3 in HER2-low breast cancer. The spin-out has presented pre-clinical data showing complete tumour regression in 3 of 5 PDX mouse models and claims a novel cleavable linker chemistry reduces off-target toxicity compared to trastuzumab deruxtecan (T-DXd). Write a structured Due Diligence Report (700 words) covering: (1) Technology Assessment — evaluate the HER3 target rationale (expression in HER2-low population, therapeutic window versus normal tissue expression), the novelty and freedom-to-operate of the linker chemistry versus AstraZeneca/Daiichi Sankyo T-DXd patents; (2) Data Quality — identify three questions you would ask about the PDX study (Which PDX models, what is the HER3 expression level in each, were the studies blinded, what are the error bars on the tumour volume curves?); (3) Manufacturing Readiness — assess whether the antibody has been produced at >10g scale under GMP, DAR (drug-to-antibody ratio) homogeneity by HIC-HPLC, and stability data at 4°C and -80°C over 18 months; (4) Competitive Landscape — map against patritumab deruxtecan (HER3-DXd, Daiichi Sankyo Phase III) and ELU001 (Elucida Oncology); (5) Risk Assessment and Recommendation — overall go/no-go recommendation with three conditions precedent for licensing. Professional business-science tone.","tags":["translational-research","industry-academia","ADC","HER3","due-diligence"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:40:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-94","title":"Precision Medicine Implementation: Biomarker-Stratified Trial Design","prompt":"You are a precision oncology clinical trial designer at a National Cancer Institute-designated cancer centre developing a Phase II biomarker-stratified trial for a PI3Kα inhibitor (alpelisib analogue) in patients with PIK3CA-mutant solid tumours. The trial uses a master protocol umbrella design with four parallel cohorts: (1) PIK3CA hotspot mutations (H1047R, E545K, E542K) by ctDNA liquid biopsy; (2) PIK3CA non-hotspot mutations; (3) PIK3CA amplification by FISH; (4) PTEN loss by IHC. Write the Biomarker Strategy section of the trial protocol (600 words) covering: companion diagnostic qualification plan (seeking FDA breakthrough device designation for the ctDNA assay — describe the pre-submission meeting process), prospective versus retrospective biomarker designation (all four stratifications are prospective co-primary biomarkers per FDA guidance on adaptive enrichment designs), concordance testing between ctDNA and archival tissue biopsy for PIK3CA mutations (required before ctDNA can replace tissue as the enrolment criterion), planned co-primary endpoints per cohort (ORR by RECIST 1.1, progression-free survival), and statistical design (Simon 2-stage for each cohort, H0: ORR ≤ 10%, H1: ORR ≥ 25%, alpha 0.10, power 80%). Discuss FISH-negative/PTEN-loss concordance as an exploratory endpoint.","tags":["translational-research","precision-medicine","biomarker","umbrella-trial","PI3K"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:45:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-95","title":"Health Economics: Cost-Effectiveness Analysis Framework","prompt":"You are a health economist at NICE (National Institute for Health and Care Excellence) advising a manufacturer preparing a Technology Appraisal submission for a new subcutaneous IL-17A inhibitor (secukinumab biosimilar) for adults with active ankylosing spondylitis (axial SpA) who have failed two TNF inhibitors. Write the Health Economics Analysis Plan (HEAP) section (600 words) covering: (1) Model structure — describe a partitioned survival model with three health states (active disease on treatment, sustained response, and treatment discontinuation/best supportive care) and justify this structure over a Markov state-transition model for this indication; (2) Effectiveness data — specify the network meta-analysis (NMA) approach to synthesise indirect comparison data against IL-17A inhibitors (ixekizumab, bimekizumab) where no head-to-head trial exists, using BASDAI 50 response as the primary outcome mapped to ASAS40; (3) Utility values — reference EQ-5D-5L UK value set tariff applied to BASDAI-stratified health states, with systematic literature review for utility inputs; (4) Costs — NHS reference costs 2022/23 for biologic administration, monitoring (CRP every 6 months, MRI sacroiliac joints annually), and adverse event costs (candidiasis management); (5) ICER calculation and threshold — the NICE threshold of £20,000-£30,000 per QALY, end-of-life modifier applicability (not applicable here), and probabilistic sensitivity analysis with 10,000 Monte Carlo iterations and tornado diagram for deterministic sensitivity analysis on the five most influential parameters.","tags":["translational-research","health-economics","NICE","ICER","ankylosing-spondylitis"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:50:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-96","title":"Interdisciplinary Collaboration: Bridging Engineering and Biology","prompt":"You are a principal investigator in biomedical engineering leading a new interdisciplinary project combining microfluidics engineering and stem cell biology to develop a vascularised kidney-on-a-chip model for drug nephrotoxicity testing. Your team includes two biomedical engineers (expertise in PDMS device fabrication and computational fluid dynamics), one stem cell biologist (iPSC-derived podocyte and tubular epithelial cell differentiation), and one pharmacologist (in vitro toxicology, LC-MS/MS biomarker measurement). Write a project Kick-Off Meeting Agenda and accompanying 'Shared Language Glossary' document (600 words combined) that: identifies three specific terminology clashes likely to cause miscommunication (e.g., 'resolution' means feature size in engineering but sensitivity in biology; 'validation' means different things in each field; 'model' is a mathematical construct in engineering but a biological system in biology), sets up a shared experimental design vocabulary, drafts the first 6-month milestones for each team member with clear interdependencies (the engineers cannot test fluid dynamics until the biologists have established cell attachment protocols; the pharmacologist cannot begin cisplatin nephrotoxicity tests until both flow conditions and cell viability baselines are established), and defines the data sharing protocol (shared OSF project page, weekly Slack updates, fortnightly in-person data review).","tags":["translational-research","interdisciplinary","organ-on-chip","kidney","biomedical-engineering"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:55:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-97","title":"Technology Readiness Level Assessment for a Medical Device","prompt":"You are a technology commercialisation manager at a university technology transfer office conducting a Technology Readiness Level (TRL) assessment for a wearable biosensor patch that continuously monitors wound exudate pH, lactate, and uric acid to detect chronic wound infection in diabetic foot ulcer patients. The device uses screen-printed electrochemical sensors on a flexible polyimide substrate with near-field communication (NFC) data transmission to a smartphone app. Conduct a structured TRL assessment from 1-9 using the European Commission Horizon Europe TRL definitions: assign the current TRL (TRL 4: technology validated in lab), and write the evidence required to progress to each subsequent level through to TRL 9 (system proven in operational environment). For the transition from TRL 5 to TRL 6 (technology demonstrated in relevant environment), specify the exact validation experiments required: bench-top testing of sensor accuracy against gold-standard clinical laboratory measurements (Siemens BM-Test Pro) at pH 5.0-8.0 in synthetic wound fluid (ASTM F1862-19 standard), lactate 0.5-10 mM, and uric acid 0.1-1.0 mM; sensor drift testing over 7-day simulated wear; and the study protocol for a first-in-human feasibility study (n=12 DFU patients, NHS REC ethics approval required). Map the commercialisation pathway against ISO 13485 medical device quality management and UKCA MDR 2002 regulatory requirements.","tags":["translational-research","medical-device","TRL","wearable-biosensor","diabetic-foot-ulcer"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T18:00:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-98","title":"One Health Research: Antimicrobial Resistance at the Human-Animal-Environment Interface","prompt":"You are a One Health epidemiologist designing a multi-sectoral surveillance study to characterise the transmission dynamics of Extended-Spectrum Beta-Lactamase (ESBL)-producing Escherichia coli across livestock (broiler chickens at four commercial farms), farm workers (n=60), and adjacent freshwater catchments (six sampling points) in a 50 km² agricultural region in the East Midlands, UK. Write the integrated study design (650 words) covering: (1) Sampling strategy — whole-genome sequencing (WGS) of 200 E. coli isolates per sector (livestock caecal swabs, farm worker rectal swabs with informed consent, water filtration concentrates at 10L per sampling event), with monthly sampling over 12 months to capture seasonal variation; (2) WGS analysis — Illumina short-read sequencing (150 bp PE, 40× coverage), de novo assembly with SPAdes, MLST using the Achtman 7-locus scheme in mlst v2.23, resistome profiling using AMRFinder+ against the NCBI AMR Reference Gene Database, plasmid replicon typing using PlasmidFinder 2.1, phylogenetic core genome SNP analysis using Roary/IQ-TREE to identify transmission clusters at <20 SNP threshold; (3) Source attribution modelling — island model or modified asymmetric island model implemented in R using the SOURCE package to estimate the probability that human isolates originate from livestock versus water versus unknown sources; (4) Ethical and regulatory considerations — farm biosecurity protocols, farm worker consent and occupational health pathway for AMR-positive participants; (5) Policy translation — reporting pathway to UK Health Security Agency (UKHSA) and Veterinary Medicines Directorate under the UK 5-Year National Action Plan on AMR.","tags":["translational-research","One-Health","antimicrobial-resistance","WGS","surveillance"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T18:05:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-99","title":"Regulatory Science: Writing a Pre-IND Meeting Request","prompt":"You are a regulatory affairs scientist at a biotechnology company preparing a Pre-IND Meeting Request to the FDA's Centre for Drug Evaluation and Research (CDER), Division of Oncology Products 1, for a first-in-class bispecific T cell engager (BiTE) targeting MSLN (mesothelin) × CD3ε for treatment of platinum-resistant ovarian cancer. Write the Pre-IND Meeting Request document following the FDA Guidance for Industry 'Formal Meetings Between the FDA and Sponsors or Applicants of PDUFA Products' (2017 guidance). The document must include: (1) Meeting Request Type B classification (pre-IND) and justification for Type B (first-in-class modality, non-standard manufacturing process); (2) Product description — bispecific format (tandem scFv, 55 kDa, produced in CHO-K1 cells, Protein A purified, >99% purity by SEC-HPLC, endotoxin <1 EU/mg); (3) Clinical development plan summary — proposed indication, Phase I dose escalation in MSLN-overexpressing ovarian cancer, patient population (platinum-resistant, ≥2 prior lines); (4) Proposed questions for FDA (8 questions on: minimum information to support IND filing, adequacy of the proposed GLP tox species selection — cynomolgus monkey with confirmed MSLN cross-reactivity — starting dose selection using MABEL approach, acceptability of the accelerated step-dose escalation scheme); (5) Background package summary referencing draft IND sections to be provided 4 weeks before the meeting. Follow FDA formatting requirements (double-spaced, 12pt Times New Roman, section headers per FDA guidance).","tags":["translational-research","regulatory-science","FDA","BiTE","pre-IND"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T18:10:00Z","featured":false},

  {"id":"sci-100","title":"Science-Industry Knowledge Exchange: Collaborative Research Agreement","prompt":"You are a knowledge exchange director at a research-intensive university drafting a Collaborative Research Agreement (CRA) between your Materials Science department and a UK-based manufacturer of lithium-sulphur (Li-S) batteries for electric vehicles. The academic team will develop novel polysulphide-trapping cathode architectures using metal-organic framework (MOF) coatings synthesised by atomic layer deposition (ALD), targeting a cycle life improvement from the current 200 cycles to >500 cycles at C/2 rate. Write the key scientific and commercial sections of the CRA (700 words): (1) Project Scope and Scientific Objectives — four specific milestones (M6: three MOF candidates with BET surface area >1500 m²/g characterised by XRD and TEM; M12: electrochemical characterisation by galvanostatic cycling in coin cells showing >350 cycles at 80% capacity retention; M18: scale-up of ALD coating to 100 cm² cathode sheets at partner's facility; M24: full pouch cell prototype with validated cycle performance); (2) Intellectual Property — background IP retention by each party, joint IP for discoveries arising from the collaboration with 50:50 ownership but exclusive commercialisation licence to the company for the energy storage field, university retains right to publish after 6-month patent review period; (3) Personnel and Resource Allocation — 1.0 FTE postdoc jointly supervised, 6-month secondment of the postdoc to the company's R&D facility in Year 2; (4) Publication Rights — all publications subject to 30-day pre-publication review for patent-sensitive content; (5) Dispute Resolution — escalation pathway (working group → PI level → executive level → independent expert arbitration under ICC rules). Reference Innovate UK KTP scheme eligibility for follow-on funding.","tags":["translational-research","industry-collaboration","CRA","lithium-sulphur","knowledge-exchange"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T18:15:00Z","featured":false}

];
